    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Associate-Full-Professor-and-Director-of-Statistical-Consulting-Centre_JR-012187-1": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor and Director of Statistical Consulting Centre",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-02\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time, Continuing Location : Parkville, Melbourne School / Faculty: School of Mathematics and Statistics, Faculty of Science Salary: Level D - $183,859 - $202,556 or Level E - $236,824 p.a. plus 17% superannuation  Prestigious leadership in statistical consulting and education Apply now to lead a nationally respected Statistical Consulting Centre and shape the future of applied statistics Benefit from outstanding employment conditions, leadership support, and professional development programs  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity   As Director of the Statistical Consulting Centre, you will integrate high-impact consulting, research and education across the University. By building partnerships with government, industry and academia, and mentoring staff and researchers, you help advance statistical practice. Strong institutional support and academic freedom enable you to shape internationally recognised research and consulting programmes.Top of FormBottom of Form   What you will deliver:   Provide strategic leadership for the Statistical Consulting Centre’s consulting, research, teaching and training activities. Oversee and grow consulting services for government, industry, business and University clients, ensuring high-quality statistical support. Lead development of innovative statistical education programs and short courses for students, researchers and external participants. Supervise and mentor academic staff and graduate researchers, fostering excellence in applied statistics and consulting practice. Build national and international collaborations that enhance the School’s research profile and real-world impact in data science. You will be a scholar of international reputation with: A research doctorate in Statistics and recognised international standing in statistical science. A demonstrated outstanding record of high-quality statistical consulting across business, industry, government and academia. Sustained, impactful research outputs and evidence of leading collaborative projects in applied or methodological statistics. Ability to communicate complex statistical concepts clearly to non-technical audiences, clients and interdisciplinary collaborators. Experience providing inclusive, strategic leadership of teams, nurturing staff development and a positive, high-performance consulting culture. Experience in high-profile consultations, such as legal reporting, expert witness roles or government advisory work. For further information please refer to the attached PD.    What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team – Statistical Consulting Centre The Statistical Consulting Centre sits within the School of Mathematics and Statistics, one of Australia’s leading schools. You will join a vibrant, research-intensive community with strong international links and major ARC-funded centres. Working closely with the Faculty of Science and partners across the University, you will support impactful, interdisciplinary research.   Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description.   Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Professor Howard Bondell at howard.bondell@unimelb.edu.au or at +61 3 8344 0169 For questions related to our recruitment process email Emily Haagensen at snr-talentacq@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: Monday 14 December 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-012187_Director of Statistical Consulting Centre PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow--Climate-Resilience-and-Adaptation-Modelling_JR-019603-2": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Climate Resilience and Adaptation Modelling",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.8 FTE); Fixed term, 21 months Faculty: Business and Economics Department/ School:  Centre for Actuarial Studies, Department of Economics Salary: Level B - $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  Transform climate risk understanding through quantitative modelling / collaborate in a global interdisciplinary lab / publish impactful research with industry partners Apply now to deliver climate resilience models that shape adaptation finance and community protection. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead quantitative modelling of climate hazards and losses within the innovative BRACE research laboratory. You will help design, develop and price adaptation and resilience instruments for catastrophe perils. You will gain rich interdisciplinary experience, working closely with academics, industry partners and funding bodies. What you will deliver:   Collaborate across BRACE work packages to develop and apply quantitative models of climate hazards and losses. Contribute to designing, developing and pricing adaptation and resilience instruments for key catastrophe perils. Lead high-quality research outputs and support competitive grant and industry funding applications across BRACE. Co-supervise Honours, Masters and PhD students while fostering a rigorous, supportive research training environment. Coordinate BRACE laboratory operations, including website content, communications, workshops and Industry Advisory Board activities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in a relevant discipline, or have all requirements completed by the commencement date. Demonstrate high-quality research through working papers, submissions or publications in refereed international journals. Communicate complex quantitative and climate concepts clearly to technical and non-technical audiences in multiple formats. Collaborate effectively in multidisciplinary teams and build strong relationships with internal and external stakeholders. Bring knowledge or experience in resilience, adaptation, climate modelling or related catastrophe risk areas. Engage confidently with industry, government or other organisations to translate research into practical applications. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Centre for Actuarial Studies You will join the Centre for Actuarial Studies within the Department of Economics. This Centre sits in the Faculty of Business and Economics and is recognised internationally for actuarial excellence. As part of the BRACE laboratory, you will work with leading academics and industry partners on catastrophe resilience. The team values rigorous research, real-world impact and collaboration, offering strong support and development opportunities. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia that are valid for the duration of this appointment. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Prof. Benjamin Avanzi at b.avanzi@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 15 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description: Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Climate Resilience and Adaption Modelling_PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow-in-the-Political-Ecology-of-Fire-and-Climate-Change-Adaptation-in-the-Philippines-and-Timor-Leste_JR-019858": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in the Political Ecology of Fire and Climate Change Adaptation in the Philippines and Timor-Leste",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time (0.8FTE); Fixed-term for 12 months  Faculty: Faculty of Science  Department/ School: School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Science  Salary: Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super   Pioneering research exploring fire politics and climate adaptation / immersive fieldwork alongside Indigenous communities in the Philippines and Timor-Leste / collaborative, interdisciplinary team within a world-leading science faculty. Build your research career while deepening critical understandings of fire, livelihoods and climate resilience through research with real-world relevance. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   In this role, you will lead critical ethnographic research exploring fire, livelihoods and climate adaptation in Southeast Asia. You will work alongside Indigenous and rural communities in the Philippines and Timor-Leste, engaging with and respecting local knowledge systems. You will benefit from mentoring within a supportive, interdisciplinary team while building a strong foundation for your academic career. What you will deliver:   Conduct high-quality ethnographic research on fire politics, gendered livelihoods and climate change adaptation in Southeast Asia. Produce scholarly publications, reports and presentations that advance political ecology and agrarian studies in the region. Contribute to collaborative research proposals, seminars and conferences, enhancing the School’s profile in critical human geography. Support supervision and mentoring of students and junior researchers involved in related projects and fieldwork. Engage respectfully with Indigenous communities, NGOs and stakeholders to build equitable, long-term research partnerships. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold, or be near completing, a PhD in human geography, anthropology, development studies or related fields. Apply advanced ethnographic methods and political ecology frameworks to analyse agrarian change and fire-related practices. Demonstrate deep respect for Indigenous knowledge systems and experience working collaboratively with rural communities. Communicate effectively in English and local languages, or show commitment to learning Filipino, Tetum and Indigenous languages. Manage complex fieldwork logistics, competing priorities and deadlines while delivering agreed research outputs with minimal supervision. Publish in relevant international journals and contribute constructively to interdisciplinary, cross-cultural research teams and partnerships. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Science   You will join the School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Sciences, a vibrant hub uniting social and physical sciences to tackle urgent environmental challenges. The School’s researchers span human geography, geoscience and atmospheric science, with recognised strengths in climate change, environmental hazards, Indigenous knowledge and space, place and social change. Within the Faculty of Science, you will benefit from world-class facilities, diverse research networks and a strong commitment to reconciliation, equity and inclusion, creating a supportive environment to develop your academic career.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .  Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.  Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.  Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Prof. Wolfram Dressler at wolfram.dressler@unimelb.edu.au   For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').  Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close:  30 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019858 - Research Fellow in the Political Ecology of Fire and Climate Change Adaptation in the Philippines and Timor-Leste - PD.docx JR-019858 - Research Fellow in the Political Ecology of Fire and Climate Change Adaptation in the Philippines and Timor-Leste - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Bioinformatician--AusTrakka-_JR-019676": {
      "title": "Bioinformatician (AusTrakka)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Fixed-term (12 months)  Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Department/ School:  Department of Microbiology and Immunology, School of Biomedical Sciences  Salary:  $111,275 – $120,435 p.a plus 17% super    Nationally significant pathogen genomics role / Drive real-time outbreak detection and response / Build connections across Australia’s public health genomics community   Apply your bioinformatics expertise to AusTrakka and help shape Australia’s future public health surveillance capability   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.    The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    You will apply advanced bioinformatics skills to national pathogen genomics surveillance through AusTrakka, directly informing outbreak detection and public health responses. Working with leading experts across jurisdictions, you will analyse and interpret genomic data, refine pipelines, and support continuous improvement of Australia’s genomics capability. You will gain deep exposure to the full public health genomics ecosystem, building relationships with key partners across government, laboratories and academia.   What you will deliver:     Plan, execute and report routine and ad hoc AusTrakka genomic analyses that support surveillance and outbreak investigations   Collaborate with genomic epidemiologists and public health partners to interpret findings and communicate clear, actionable reports   Design, develop, validate and maintain bioinformatics pipelines and workflows for routine pathogen genomics service delivery   Maintain high standards for data management, documentation, turnaround times and audit-ready practice across analyses and pipelines   Represent AusTrakka and MDU PHL bioinformatics capabilities in internal and external forums, including meetings and conferences   You may be a great fit if you:     Hold a postgraduate qualification in Bioinformatics, Computational Biology or a closely related discipline   Use Python and/or R confidently and work effectively in a UNIX or Linux environment   Design, validate and troubleshoot bioinformatics pipelines using standard tools for assembly, gene detection and phylogenetics   Work with large next-generation sequencing datasets, including phylogenomics, ideally in a public health or clinical context   Manage competing priorities and high-throughput analyses while maintaining accuracy, documentation quality and required turnaround times   Bring experience or interest in workflow engines, containers, and advanced statistical or microbial genomics approaches   For further information please refer to the attached PD.    What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:    Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team    You will join the Microbiological Diagnostic Unit Public Health Laboratory within the Department of Microbiology and Immunology, part of the School of Biomedical Sciences in the Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences. Embedded in the Peter Doherty Institute, the team integrates laboratory science, genomics, epidemiology and bioinformatics to tackle diseases of public health importance. You will collaborate with Victorian, national and international partners in a values-driven environment that prizes excellence, integrity, inclusivity and real-world impact.   Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .    Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.    A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.    Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria   Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Jake Lacey at jake.lacey@unimelb.edu.au      For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: ​22 November 2026​ 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:     JR-019676 Bioinformatician (AusTrakka) -PD.docx   JR-019676 Bioinformatician (AusTrakka) -PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Lecturer-in-Experimental-High-Energy-Physics_JR-019011-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Experimental High Energy Physics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing Faculty: Faculty of Science Department/ School: School of Physics Salary: Level B: $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super (retirement savings system/pension scheme)   Join Australia’s largest experimental particle physics group, which has led two successive ARC Centres of Excellence and has roles in Belle II (KeK), Hyper-Kamiokande, ATLAS (CERN) and dark matter direct detection at SUPL. The position is for a experimental particle physicist ready to build an independent research program, ideally in flavour or neutrino physics. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.      The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity The role balances research, teaching and service, with time commitments typically in the ratio 40:40:20. We particularly welcome applicants in flavour or neutrino physics, with experience on any leading experimental program; strong candidates in other areas of experimental particle physics will also be considered. You will be expected to set your own research direction. The School will support you with mentoring, a start-up funding package and a reduced teaching allocation in order to establish your research program.   What you will deliver: Deliver, develop and coordinate undergraduate and graduate physics subjects, including specialised content in particle physics, through lectures, tutorials and laboratory classes. Lead research within large international collaborations, taking visible roles in detector development, operations or physics analysis, and publishing and presenting the results. Build an independent record of competitive grant success and expand national and international collaborative networks and partnerships. Contribute to School planning, committees and professional activities while promoting University values, diversity and integrity. A competitive applicant will: Hold a PhD in experimental particle physics or a closely related discipline. Bring outstanding ability and experience in any area of experimental particle physics. Have hands-on expertise in detector instrumentation, data analysis or scientific computing. Demonstrate an emerging independent research profile, with a publication record and leadership experience commensurate with opportunity, and a clear plan for a research program that complements the existing group. Have experience co-supervising or mentoring research students. Collaborate constructively within large international experiments, showing excellent communication and relationship-building skills.   For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – School of Physics The Experimental Particle Physics group has four academics, 5 postdocs, 15 PhD students and runs local detector laboratories, a clean room and has access to cryogenic and detectors facilities in the Stawell underground Laboratory. It led the ARC Centre of Excellence for Particle Physics at the Terascale (CoEPP) and leads the ARC Centre of Excellence for Dark Matter Particle Physics. Flavour: a founding member of the Belle II experiment at the SuperKEKB electron–positron collider at KEK, Japan, with leading roles in physics analysis, Silicon Vertex Detector construction, grid computing and collaboration governance. Physics interests include CKM matrix elements, new physics in (semi)leptonic B decays, CP violation and lepton flavour violation. Neutrinos: Melbourne leads Australia’s participation in Hyper-Kamiokande, the next-generation water Cherenkov neutrino detector under construction in Japan, including a contribution to the outer detector and on-site construction from 2027. Our physics goals include leptonic CP violation, the neutrino mass ordering and the search for proton decay. Dark matter: the group leads Australia’s dark matter direct-detection program, including the SABRE South experiment and a cryogenic facility at the Stawell Underground Physics Laboratory (SUPL), Australia’s first deep underground laboratory. Other programs: ATLAS at CERN, where the group had a leading role in the analysis of Higgs boson decays to fermions, dark matter and multi-fermion final states searches. It contributes to the ATLAS Inner Tracker (ITk) silicon upgrade.   Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria   Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Professor Harry Quiney at quiney@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 30 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description:              JR-019011 - Lecturer in Experimental High Energy Physics - PD.docx JR-019011 - Lecturer in Experimental High Energy Physics - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Retail-Officer_JR-019373-1": {
      "title": "Retail Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing Department/School: Chancellery – Advancement, Communications & Marketing Salary: UOM 3 - $69,554 - $76,795 p.a. plus 17% superannuation Working hours: Full-time; typically 10am–6pm Monday–Friday, with limited out-of-hours event shifts Professional campus retail in a leading university / engage students, staff and senior stakeholders / mix of store, online and event work Bring your customer service expertise to a professional retail setting and apply now, including a short video interview. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity You will deliver high-quality customer experiences in the University’s flagship Merchandise Shop and campus retail activations. You will interact with students, professional staff and senior executives in a polished, corporate-style retail environment. You will work primarily Monday to Friday, with occasional evening or weekend shifts during major events. What you will deliver: Provide proactive, high-quality customer service in-store, by phone and email across a diverse university community. Drive merchandise sales through confident product recommendations, upselling and maintaining strong visual merchandising standards. Process POS transactions, online orders and stock updates accurately, ensuring secure payments and reliable inventory records. Support day-to-day store operations, supervising casual staff on shift and coordinating pop-up shops during key events. Uphold compliance, safety and continuous improvement practices, contributing ideas to enhance customer and stakeholder experience.   You may be a great fit if you: Bring demonstrated experience in retail or customer service, including face-to-face sales in a busy environment. Operate POS systems confidently, with accurate cash handling, reconciliation and end-of-day balancing. Use desktop applications and business systems to enter data, manage orders and maintain stock or inventory records. Apply visual merchandising skills to create attractive, commercial displays that support sales and brand standards. Balance multiple tasks with strong attention to detail, organisation and adherence to established procedures and guidelines. Understand workplace safety and loss-prevention principles within a customer-facing retail or service setting. For further information, please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – Advancement, Communications and Marketing Advancement, Communications and Marketing supports the University’s mission by showcasing education, research and community impact globally. You will join the Strategy, Operations and Engagement portfolio, working in the flagship on-campus Merchandise Shop. The team is collaborative, professional and people-focused, offering varied work across store, online and event settings. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. As part of the selection process, shortlisted candidates may be asked to complete a brief video interview via an external provider undefined Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Alex Spezza at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 8th of November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: JR-019373 - Retail Officer - PD.pdf JR-019373 - Retail Officer - PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Senior-Lecturer_JR-018973-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing Faculty: Business and Economics Department/ School: Department of Accounting Salary: Level C - $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. plus 17% super  World-leading accounting department / Shape curricula and research with global impact / Strong professional and industry engagement Join a globally respected accounting department and advance your research, teaching, and professional influence—apply now. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   As a Senior Lecturer in Accounting, you will advance high‑impact accounting research and research training — publish in top international journals. You will transform student learning, designing bold, evidence‑based teaching, and support student wellbeing. You will also translate research into practice through external partnerships. What you will deliver:   Conduct high-quality accounting research, targeting publication in premier international journals and contributing to the Department’s reputation. Lead curriculum design and innovation, developing contemporary course materials and integrating accounting with emerging technologies and contexts. Deliver engaging lectures, tutorials, and seminars across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, including subject and course coordination. Contribute to departmental governance, committees, professional engagement, and student consultation, supporting a high-quality learning environment. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a completed PhD in accounting Demonstrate a strong publication record, including articles in premier international refereed accounting or auditing journals. Provide evidence of high-quality tertiary teaching in accounting or auditing, including honours and postgraduate levels. Lead teaching and research initiatives, supervising honours and research higher degree students and mentoring emerging scholars. Engage actively with accounting professional bodies, firms, or the broader business community to enrich programmes. Contribute collegially to departmental administration, committees, and broader university initiatives, aligning with equal opportunity principles. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Department of Accounting The Department of Accounting is one of the academic departments within the Faculty of Business and Economics (FBE) at the University of Melbourne. The Department is committed to excellence in research and teaching across the core areas of the discipline, serving a diverse community of students and researchers.  The Department of Accounting hosts research profiles across multiple disciplines, including management accounting, financial accounting, auditing and assurance, and accounting information systems. The Department maintains strong global connections to world-class research through the seminar series (featuring invited international speakers) and regional conferences, such as the Asia-Pacific Management Accounting Conference. Additionally, the department is actively affiliated with professional bodies and industry networks through the Centre for Accounting & Industry Partnerships (CAIP). Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Prof. Jennifer Grafton - j.grafton@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 28 February 2027 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone (AEDT).  Position Description:   Word : JR_018973_Senior Lecturer_PD.docx PDF : JR_018973_Senior Lecturer_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer-in-Islamic-Studies--Education-Focused-_JR-019622": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Islamic Studies (Education Focused)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time (1 FTE); Fixed-term for 11 months Faculty: The Faculty of Arts Department/ School: Asia Institute Salary: Level B $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  This education-focused Lecturer role in Islamic Studies supports a multidisciplinary program and strong academic, community engagement. Apply to shape inclusive Islamic Studies teaching, coordinate subjects and contribute to scholarly and community engagement. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will teach Islamic Studies within a multidisciplinary Islamic Studies program at the Asia Institute. You will coordinate subjects, design inclusive curricula and develop technology-enhanced learning resources for diverse student cohorts.You will also contribute to scholarly and engagement activities that connect Oman, Australia and the wider academic community. What you will deliver:   Coordinate and deliver subjects in Islamic Studies, including lectures, tutorials, seminars and assessment. Provide student consultation, manage assessment extensions, address academic integrity issues and assist with special consideration processes. Participate in Boards of Examiners, program and School meetings, committees and other relevant fora. Engage in pedagogical scholarship, professional development and service activities that support inclusive, high-quality teaching practice. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a completed PhD in Islamic Studies. Bring broad knowledge of Islamic Studies, including Qur'anic Studies, contemporary Muslim societies, politics and related areas. Demonstrate effective tertiary teaching, including subject coordination, lecturing, tutoring and inclusive classroom practices. Develop educational resources and coordinate subjects in Islamic Studies, using strong computer literacy and eTeaching approaches. Maintain an emerging record of pedagogical or academic research that informs and enhances teaching practice. Apply excellent organisational skills, policy understanding and commitment to inclusive learning for diverse student cohorts.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – The Asia Institute You will join the Asia Institute, the University’s key centre for Asian languages, cultures and societies. Our academic staff provide leadership in studying the intellectual, legal, politico-economic, cultural and religious traditions of Asia. Within the Faculty of Arts, you will work alongside leading humanities scholars committed to diversity, inclusion and community engagement. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact A/Prof Muhammad Kamal at muhammad@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Petra Newton at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 15th November at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019622 Lecturer in Islamic Studies (Education Focused) PD.docx JR-019622 Lecturer in Islamic Studies (Education Focused) PD.pdf"
