    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Project-Manager---XR-Innovation-Lab_JR-019378": {
      "title": "Project Manager – XR Innovation Lab",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time (0.2 FTE); Fixed-term (12 months) - Maternity leave backfill Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Department/School: Centre for Youth Mental Health  Salary: Level B: $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. pro rata plus 17% super    Pivotal role in a cutting-edge XR Innovation Lab / improve mental health outcomes for young people / collaborate with leading researchers, clinicians and technologists  Join a dynamic multidisciplinary team to drive XR projects from concept to impact in youth mental health services.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.      The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    You will coordinate and shape a pioneering XR Innovation Lab focused on transforming youth mental health services.  You will work closely with researchers, clinicians, technologists and lived experience experts to deliver high-impact projects.  You will strengthen agile ways of working and help translate XR innovations into real-world clinical settings.   What you will deliver:    Coordinate Lab meetings, strategy days and collaborative activities, ensuring clear actions, follow-up and shared priorities.  Support project leads with planning, risk management, regulatory compliance and budget oversight across multiple XR initiatives.  Implement and maintain project and asset management systems, including agile tools such as Jira and Trello.  Develop web content, reports, presentations and demonstrations that showcase Lab activities to internal and external stakeholders.  Manage the Lab inbox and VR space bookings, enabling effective access and use by staff and visitors.  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a relevant tertiary qualification or equivalent experience in project management, research, health, innovation or technology.  Demonstrate experience coordinating projects and priorities across multidisciplinary teams in fast-paced environments.  Bring strong organisational skills, managing competing demands and consistently following through on agreed actions.  Communicate confidently with diverse stakeholders and facilitate effective meetings, workshops and collaborative activities.  Use Microsoft Office and project management or collaboration platforms, ideally including agile tools like Jira or Trello.  Offer experience with XR, VR, digital health, youth mental health or innovation-focused research environments  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team – Centre for Youth Mental Health   You will join the Centre for Youth Mental Health, co-located with Orygen, a world-leading youth mental health organisation.  Our XR Innovation Lab unites researchers, clinicians, technologists and lived experience experts to create transformative digital interventions.  You will be part of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences, with rich collaborations and a vibrant research culture.   Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria   Want to know more?   For questions related to our recruitment process email Vanessa Phan at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: 11 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    PD_Project Manager – XR Innovation Lab.docx   PD_Project Manager – XR Innovation Lab.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Graduate-Researcher-Academic-Associate---School-of-Electrical--Mechanical-and-Infrastructure-Engineering_JR-019623": {
      "title": "Graduate Researcher Academic Associate - School of Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\nRole type:   Part-time (min. FTE: 0.2 max. FTE 0.3); fixed-term 24-months, tied to candidature Faculty: Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology Department/ School: School of Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering Salary: Level A.2 $92,243 (pro rata for part-time) p.a. plus 17% super  New opportunity to gain meaningful academic experience in education within the Department of Infrastructure Engineering in a vibrant School of Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering. Undertake career advancing professional development, including minimum 30 hours paid professional development Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. About the role   The School of Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering invites applications for two new Graduate Researcher Academic Associate (GRAA) positions from graduate researchers currently enrolled in the Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology with discipline and domain knowledge as required in the following subject areas: Position 1 (Transport Engineering): CVEN90075 Transport Infrastructure Design CVEN90048 Transport Systems Position 2 (Geomatics): GEOM30009 Imaging the Environment GEOM90007 Information Visualisation As a GRAA, the position will contribute positively, both individually and as part of a team, to educating, inspiring and supporting students. The GRAA will work directly with the teaching team and be responsible for carrying out education related activities for the mentioned subjects. Key education responsibilities will include: Delivery of sessional teaching activities (which includes tutorial, laboratory experiments and field work) in relevant Department discipline subjects Development of teaching resources for both Undergraduate and Masters subjects Other Academic activities, including student consultations, assessment feedback, monitoring discussion board and marking Improving the Accessibility and Inclusivity of teaching materials and the Canvas Learning Management System In addition, you will be supported to participate as a valued academic member of the School in contributing to the School’s academic mission, life and community and supported to engage in at least 30 hours of paid professional development. Who we are looking for We are seeking applicants enrolled as a University of Melbourne student undertaking a graduate research degree (either Doctor of Philosophy or other research doctorate) of the Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology with discipline and domain knowledge in the following areas: Transport Engineering (and the above-mentioned subject areas) Geomatics (and the above-mentioned subject areas) You will be a passionate educator and have the ability to contribute positively, both individually and as part of a team, to educating, inspiring and supporting students. You will also have excellent written and verbal communication and interpersonal skills and excellent organisational skills with the ability to manage one’s own time effectively, administer multiple tasks simultaneously and meet deadlines. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team You will join the Department of Infrastructure Engineering within the School of Electrical, Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering. The department combines civil, environmental and geomatics engineering, focusing on sustainable solutions to complex infrastructure challenges. You will work in a vibrant, research-active environment with strong industry partnerships and interdisciplinary collaborations. The Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology champions impactful education and supports your growth as an academic engineer. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please obtain and upload a signed support letter from your PhD supervisor. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Prof Stephan Winter at winter@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Petra Newton at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 15th November at 23:55 Melbourne Time Position Description:   JR-019623 Graduate Research Academic Associate PD.docx JR-019623 Graduate Research Academic Associate PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Facilities-Specialist---Kenneth-Myer-Building_JR-019558-1": {
      "title": "Facilities Specialist – Kenneth Myer Building",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing  Department: Campus Operations & Delivery, Enterprise Services Group  Salary: UOM 7 - $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% super  Unique facilities leadership in a flagship research building / blend technical expertise with stakeholder-focused service / drive safety, continuity and asset performance  Apply now to shape a high-impact research environment by optimising building performance, safety and stakeholder experience.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   You will lead facilities and building services maintenance activities for the Kenneth Myer Building’s complex research environment. As the principal contact for maintenance and minor works, you will safeguard safety, compliance and research continuity. You will build strong partnerships with diverse stakeholders while driving continuous improvement in asset performance and building operations.  What you will deliver:   Ensure building structure, services and plant operate safely and reliably by monitoring BAS and coordinating maintenance activities.  Coordinate reactive and planned works across PC2 laboratories and specialist spaces to minimise disruption and protect research outcomes.  Lead stakeholder communication, managing and maintaining stakeholder relationships, balancing competing priorities, identifying operational improvements and promoting a culture of continuous improvement.  Manage contractors, budgets and invoices to deliver compliant, cost-effective maintenance and minor projects aligned with lease obligations.  Maintain accurate engineering records, asset plans and risk controls, including emergency management roles and governance reporting.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold an engineering degree or equivalent facilities management experience in complex, highly serviced building environments.  Demonstrate strong contractor supervision, performance monitoring and compliance with OHS, risk management and procurement frameworks.  Possess broad technical aptitude across mechanical, electrical, hydraulic and laboratory services, including fault diagnosis and root cause analysis.  Understand bioresources and containment facilities, regulatory requirements and associated environmental, health and safety considerations.  Use digital control systems, BAS and information management tools confidently, with excellent record keeping and report writing capability.  Manage multiple priorities, budgets and stakeholder expectations effectively, driving continuous improvement and sound financial decision-making.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team:   Campus Operations & Delivery sits within the Enterprise Services Group, supporting over 600 buildings across the University’s campuses. The team ensures safe, reliable and innovative facilities that enable exceptional teaching, research and community experiences. Within this portfolio, you will focus on the Kenneth Myer Building, a flagship, multidisciplinary research environment. You will collaborate with facilities, asset management and capital works specialists who value partnership, professionalism and continuous improvement. Together, you will shape high-performing places that reflect the University’s ambition for world-class campus stewardship.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application.   Want to know more?   For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: Tuesday 10 November ​ 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description: PD_JR-019558 Facilities Specialist – Kenneth Myer Building.docx PD_JR-019558 Facilities Specialist – Kenneth Myer Building.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Laser-Ablation--Automation-and-Digitisation-Research-Scientist_JR-018485-1": {
      "title": "Laser Ablation, Automation and Digitisation Research Scientist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing (Research Contingent)  Faculty: Faculty of Science  Department/School: School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Sciences  Salary: Level of appointment is subject to qualification and experience Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super (PhD entry level $110,319) Level B: $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super Advance state-of-the-art laser ablation and automated analytical geochemistry capabilities in a world-class research environment. Create new ICP-MS methods and build capacity at the hardware-software interface, linking novel research capability and capacity building to translational research outcomes and impact. Lead the development of automated and digitised analytical geochemistry capabilities, advancing research and collaboration across disciplines and fostering new R&D partnerships and initiatives. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will advance analytical geochemistry by developing laboratory, automation and digital workflows that generate new and improved research capabilities and build capacity within Melbourne Analytical Geochemistry and across the Melbourne Isotope Analytics Research Platform. Combining specialist analytical expertise with digital innovation, you will help develop new methods, improve data stewardship and accessibility, and build research infrastructure that enables interdisciplinary collaboration. In addition to driving R&D, you will also contribute to research partnerships, scholarly outputs and community engagement, helping translate advances in analytical geochemistry into broader research and societal outcomes. What you will deliver:   Develop, optimise and document laboratory, analytical and digital workflows for laser ablation and solution-based mass spectrometric analyses. Advance automation, data reduction, quality assurance, imaging and digital pipelines to generate robust, reusable and well-curated research datasets. Troubleshoot and improve analytical instrumentation and digital infrastructure, developing new protocols and research capabilities in response to evolving research needs. Collaborate with researchers and external partners across academia, government and industry, contributing to research projects, funding proposals, scholarly outputs and intellectual property development. Train and mentor students and researchers, develop standard operating procedures, and communicate research capabilities and outcomes to scientific and wider communities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in trace element and/or isotope geochemistry or a related discipline, or demonstrate equivalent expertise. Bring experience in analytical geochemistry and laboratory protocols, alongside demonstrated digital capabilities in areas such as automated QA/QC, data reduction, imaging, data curation or programming. Enjoy troubleshooting technical challenges and developing or improving analytical methods, digital workflows and research infrastructure. Work effectively both independently and collaboratively, building productive relationships across multidisciplinary research teams and with academic, industry and government partners. Bring strong communication and organisational skills, with an interest in developing an academic specialist career through research outputs, training and engagement with scientific and wider communities. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Sciences   You will join the School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Sciences within the vibrant Faculty of Science. The Melbourne Isotope Analytics Research Platform and its MAG facility blend cutting-edge analytical infrastructure with interdisciplinary scientific collaborations. You will work alongside experts in geoscience, environmental science and archaeological science, addressing questions across time and space.  The team values curiosity, inclusion and impact, supporting flexible work and professional growth in a collaborative environment. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .  Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.  Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.  Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Brandon Mahan at brandon.mahan@unimelb.edu.au   For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').  Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 6 December 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018485 - Laser Ablation, Automation and Digitisation Research Scientist - PD.docx JR-018485 - Laser Ablation, Automation and Digitisation Research Scientist - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Enterprise-Professor-Fellow---Infrastructure-and-Transport-Platform_JR-017552": {
      "title": "Enterprise Professor/Fellow - Infrastructure and Transport Platform",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time (0.6 FTE), Fixed-term for 2 years Faculty: Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology Department: Office of the Dean Salary: Level D: $183,859 - $202,556 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% super Level E: $236,824 p.a. (pro-rata) plus 17% super High-profile, industry-facing academic role / Bridge infrastructure and transport sectors with FEIT research / Shape impactful strategic partnerships. Apply your senior sector experience to build influential collaborations that advance infrastructure, transport, and university research impact. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead industry engagement for FEIT’s Infrastructure and Transport Platform, matching sector needs with world-class research capabilities. You will use your networks and executive experience to shape strategic partnerships that deliver commercial value and societal impact. You will also mentor platform staff and support the Platform Leader to grow a vibrant, industry-connected ecosystem. What you will deliver:   Forge strategic partnerships between infrastructure and transport organisations and FEIT researchers to address complex sector challenges. Identify and scope collaboration opportunities, including proposals and white papers aligned with sector priorities and FEIT capabilities. Champion the Infrastructure and Transport Platform at industry events, strengthening visibility, reputation, and stakeholder relationships. Mentor and coach platform staff to build capability in industry engagement and partnership development. Advise and support the Platform Leader on business development strategy, pipelines, and resource focus for maximum impact. You may be a great fit if you:   Bring extensive infrastructure and/or transport sector leadership experience and strong credibility with senior industry stakeholders. Demonstrate a strong track record in business development, partnership creation, and delivering mutually beneficial commercial outcomes. Maintain a contemporary, influential professional network and confidently leverage relationships to achieve strategic objectives. Understand current and emerging research challenges, regulations, and compliance trends across infrastructure and transport sectors. Offer senior executive experience within public or private organisations, with proven ability to lead and mentor others. Meet the Level E criteria through eminent industry leadership and recognition, or be ready to progress from Level D. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology You will join the Faculty of Engineering and Information Technology, a national leader in engineering and IT education and research. Working within the Infrastructure and Transport Platform, you will collaborate with experts across engineering, construction, mobility, logistics, and asset management. The team partners with industry, government, and communities to tackle real-world challenges and deliver transformational impact aligned with FEIT 2035 and the University’s Strategy 2030. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position and to our recruitment process, email Shabon Nawabi at Shabon.nawabi@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Tuesday 24 November 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-017552 Enterprise Professor Fellow Infrastructure and Transport Platform Position Description.pdf JR-017552 Enterprise Professor Fellow Infrastructure and Transport Platform Position Description Accessible.doc"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Fellow-or-Senior-Research-Fellow--Indigenous-Health-Equity_JR-019470-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow or Senior Research Fellow, Indigenous Health Equity",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time 1 FTE OR Part Time (0.6FTE to 1.0FTE); Fixed term available for  2  years  Faculty: Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Department/ School:  Melbourne School of Public Health - Onemda, Indigenous Health Equity Unit  Salary: Level B or Level C  Level B: $124,656 - $148,023 per annum (pro rata for part-time) plus 17% super   Level C: $152,695 - $176,065 per annum (pro rata for part-time) plus 17% super    Aboriginal-led research environment / tackle intergenerational trauma and perinatal wellbeing / advance Indigenous health equity nationally  Apply now to shape trauma-informed, culturally safe research that improves outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander families.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic).     The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    You will join an Aboriginal-led team advancing Indigenous health equity through impactful perinatal trauma and family wellbeing research. You will lead and support mixed-methods projects that improve support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander parents. You will strengthen your leadership, supervision and knowledge translation capabilities while working closely with communities and policy makers.   What you will deliver:    Lead and coordinate research on intergenerational trauma, perinatal wellbeing and strategies to improve Indigenous health equity.  Produce high-quality peer-reviewed publications and diverse knowledge translation outputs for academic, policy and community audiences.  Supervise and mentor junior staff and graduate students, guiding ethics, study design, project management and stakeholder engagement.  Contribute to teaching, curriculum development and seminars within the Indigenous Health Equity Unit and School.  Engage with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, services and policymakers to support trauma-informed, culturally safe practice.  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a PhD or equivalent training in public health, epidemiology, allied health, psychology or related fields.  Bring research experience in public health and/or Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health, including translational activities.  Demonstrate experience working respectfully with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, organisations and service providers.  Apply strong quantitative and qualitative data collection and analysis skills, using relevant analytical and statistical software.  Manage complex research projects with excellent organisation, deadline management and evidence of effective team collaboration.  Communicate clearly in writing and verbally with diverse stakeholders across research, policy, clinical and community settings.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team – Melbourne School of Public Health   You will join Onemda in the Melbourne School of Public Health, focused on Indigenous wellbeing. The Indigenous Health Equity Unit leads innovative, community-driven research, teaching and policy translation to improve Indigenous health. You will collaborate with Aboriginal leaders and multidisciplinary experts in a supportive, culturally safe, community-partnered environment.   Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description. (maximum of 300 words for each criteria). Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria   Want to know more?   For questions related to our recruitment process email Vanessa Phan at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​13 November 2026​ 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    PD_Identified_Research Fellow_Senior Research Fellow Indigenous Health Equity.docx   PD_Identified_Research Fellow_Senior Research Fellow Indigenous Health Equity.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Fellow--Carbon-Mineralization_JR-019399-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow, Carbon Mineralization",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-Term for 18 Months  Faculty: Faculty of Science  Department/ School: School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Sciences Salary: Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super (PhD entry level $110,319 p.a.)    Innovative net zero research combining hands-on DAC-MIN pilot plant experimentation and modelling.  Join a world-class team advancing carbon mineralisation technologies to support Australia's transition to a net zero future.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.       The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity   In this role, you will drive experimental and modelling research to advance ex situ carbon mineralisation technologies. You will operate and optimise a pioneering DAC-MIN pilot plant, applying scientific expertise to help develop practical solutions for reducing carbon emissions.  What you will deliver:    Design and conduct laboratory experiments and geochemical modelling to advance ex situ carbon mineralisation processes.  Develop and refine methods for cation leaching, impurity removal, and carbonate precipitation from industrial waste materials.  Operate, monitor and optimise the carbon mineralisation pilot plant, ensuring safe, efficient, and reliable performance.  Publish high-impact research, present at conferences, and contribute to competitive funding proposals and industry partnerships.  Co-supervise and mentor graduate and PhD students, supporting a collaborative, inclusive research culture within the School.  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold, or be near completing, a PhD in hydrogeochemistry, industrial chemistry, chemical engineering, or related discipline.  Demonstrate research experience in fluid–rock reactions, carbon mineralisation, or water treatment, including experimental or modelling work.  Present a strong, peer-reviewed publication record in relevant fields, commensurate with career stage and opportunities.  Bring proven project or laboratory management skills, ensuring safe, efficient delivery of complex experimental programmes.  Collaborate effectively in multi-disciplinary teams and communicate complex concepts clearly to diverse academic and industry stakeholders.  Contribute to student supervision, mentoring, and the development of competitive research funding applications and collaborations.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program     For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Sciences   Joining the School of Geography, Earth and Atmospheric Sciences, you will work within a vibrant, multi-disciplinary community.  The School unites strengths in geography, geoscience and atmospheric science, with leading expertise in climate change, environmental hazards and Earth system interactions.  As part of the Faculty of Science, you will access world-class facilities, diverse research networks and strong industry partnerships. You will be supported by an inclusive culture that values Indigenous knowledge, collaboration and impactful science for a better future.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process .   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria     Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Prof. Ralf Haese at ralf.haese@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').     Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: 19 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:   JR-019399 - Research Fellow, Carbon Mineralization - PD.docx JR-019399 - Research Fellow, Carbon Mineralization - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Workday-System-Support-Specialist---HCM_JR-017112-1": {
      "title": "Workday System Support Specialist, HCM",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing Department/School: Chief Information Officer Group Salary: UOM 7: $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% superannuation Specialist Workday HCM role driving optimisation / collaborate with HR and IT / enhance people systems University-wide Apply now to lead Workday HCM support, optimise HR processes, and uplift system capability across the University. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will support and enhance Workday HCM to improve HR processes University-wide. You will translate business needs into scalable configurations, driving system reliability, compliance and a better user experience. You will collaborate with HR, IT and vendors, gaining broad exposure to projects and continuous improvement. What you will deliver: Act as the Workday Talent & Performance SME, configuring business processes, workflows and security, while providing support across Recruitment, Core HCM, Absence and Compensation modules. Lead investigation and resolution of integration and production issues, ensuring stable system performance and data integrity. Plan and coordinate testing activities, including regression and user acceptance testing, with detailed scripts and documentation. Deliver Tier 1–2 user support, training and guidance, uplifting Workday capability across HR and related teams. Collaborate with HR, Payroll, IT and vendors on enhancements, projects and reporting to meet organisational requirements.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold certified Workday Talent and Performance credentials alongside an appropriate tertiary qualification or equivalent training and experience. Demonstrate strong experience configuring, supporting and optimising Workday Talent and Performance modules. Have delivered end-to-end Workday implementations and enhancements, covering requirements gathering, design, configuration, testing, deployment, hypercare and continuous improvement. Experience supporting Workday Core HCM, Absence and Compensation, will be highly regarded. Deliver full system implementation lifecycles, covering requirements, design, configuration, testing, deployment, hypercare and enhancements. Configure Workday business processes, security, workflows and integrations, aligned with organisational structures and compliance needs. Apply deep understanding of Workday job architecture, profiles and structures to support talent management and workforce planning. Investigate and resolve complex functional and production issues using strong analytical, problem-solving and organisational skills. For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – Chief Information Officer Group You will join the Chief Information Officer Group, the University’s integrated IT function supporting teaching, research and administration. The Group designs, delivers and maintains fit-for-purpose technology solutions that keep systems secure, available and adaptable. You will collaborate with Academic Technology, Cybersecurity and Enterprise Technology teams, working closely with faculties and portfolios. Your work will contribute directly to Strategy 2030, enabling resilient operations and exceptional experiences for staff and students. You will join a supportive professional community that values innovation, collaboration and continuous improvement in digital services. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Alex Spezza at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Wednesday the18th of November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: JR-017112 - Workday System Support Specialist, HCM - PD.docx JR-017112 - Workday System Support Specialist, HCM - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Fellow-in-Circular-Economy_JR-019034": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Circular Economy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time (1.0 FTE); Fixed term for up to 2 years Faculty: Engineering and Information Technology School: Chemical and Biomedical Engineering Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. (pro rata) plus 17% super  Level B: AUD $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. (pro rata) plus 17% super  Shape interdisciplinary circular economy research / collaborate with national and global partners / drive real-world sustainability impact Apply now to grow your research career while advancing circular economy practice and policy. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will work closely with the Chair of Circular Economy to design and deliver innovative research programmes. You will build partnerships across disciplines, industry and government to translate insights into measurable sustainability outcomes. What you will deliver:   Design and deliver high-quality circular economy research projects in interdisciplinary teams with industry, government and campus partners. Identify and secure new collaborative funding opportunities, including proposals in plastics, metals, energy and related systems. Develop and manage project plans, budgets, timelines and milestones to ensure successful delivery of research programmes. Coordinate engagement activities including workshops, seminars and events that strengthen partnerships and visibility for circular economy. Contribute to advancing sustainability and circular economy at the University through publications, presentations and internal initiatives. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in a relevant field such as sustainability, circular economy or engineering. Demonstrate experience developing collaborative funding opportunities with partners in industry, government or research organisations. Work effectively within interdisciplinary research teams, building constructive relationships across diverse disciplines and stakeholder groups. Manage projects and budgets confidently, setting priorities, monitoring progress and delivering agreed outcomes on time. Communicate complex ideas clearly, with strong written, oral and workshop facilitation skills across in-person and online formats. Bring an outstanding track record in circular economy or related project areas, including independent, self-directed research (quantitative and/or qualitative). For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation. Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being.  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning. Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company.  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program.  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team Within the School of Chemical and Biomedical Engineering, you will join a vibrant, interdisciplinary research community. You will collaborate closely with the Department of Chemical Engineering, renowned for world-leading industry-engaged sustainability research. You will benefit from FEIT’s strong partnerships, mission-led 2035 strategy and major circular economy research initiatives and centres. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Brett Verity at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Position Description:   JR-019034 Research Fellow in Circular Economy PD.doc JR-019034 Research Fellow in Circular Economy PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Senior-Project-Officer_JR-018922": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time; Fixed term for 12 months   Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Department/School: Department of Microbiology and Immunology, School of Biomedical Sciences  Salary: $111,275 – $120,435 p.a. plus 17% super   Impact global pathogen surveillance / coordinate platform deployments / collaborate with diverse public health partners   Apply now to lead Trakka implementations and help transform pathogen genomics into actionable public health insight.   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.    The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    You will coordinate platform deployments that enable real time pathogen genomics for critical public health decisions.  You will partner with technical and public health teams, shaping how genomic data informs surveillance, research and biosecurity.  You will gain experience working within a leading infection and immunity institute on nationally significant digital infrastructure. What you will deliver:     Lead planning and coordination of platform deployments across various health settings.   Develop and manage implementation plans, timelines, milestones and resources to support effective platform adoption.   Coordinate stakeholder engagement, onboarding, training and user acceptance testing to ensure deployment readiness.   Monitor project progress, manage risks and issues, and report outcomes, benefits and key performance indicators.   Liaise between technical teams and end users, capturing feedback and supporting continuous platform improvement.   You may be a great fit if you:     Hold a tertiary qualification in IT, data science, public health or a closely related discipline.   Bring demonstrated experience managing complex projects, products or implementations involving IT systems or data platforms.   Demonstrate strong stakeholder engagement skills and success building relationships across diverse partner organisations.   Have experience working with information systems, digital platforms or data driven public health programmes.   Apply excellent organisational, communication and problem solving capabilities within fast paced, multidisciplinary environments.   Offer experience or strong interest in public health, genomics, bioinformatics or health data management contexts.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.    What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:    Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team   You will join the Department of Microbiology and Immunology within the School of Biomedical Sciences. Your role sits jointly in the Centre for Pathogen Genomics and the Microbiological Diagnostic Unit Public Health Laboratory. Together, these teams deliver integrated pathogen genomics, diagnostics and surveillance to support public health decision making. You will collaborate with leading experts across genomics, bioinformatics, epidemiology, laboratory science and capacity building. You will be part of an inclusive, values driven environment focused on innovation, impact and world health outcomes.     Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .    Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.    A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.    You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application     Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Tuyet Hoang at tuyet.hoang@unimelb.edu.au   For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: Sunday 8 November 1:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:     JR-018922 Senior Project Officer - PD.docx   JR-018922 Senior Project Officer - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/The-Director--Enterprise-Partnerships--Health-and-Life-Sciences-_JR-019078": {
      "title": "Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health and Life Sciences)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nDirector, Enterprise Partnerships (Health and Life Sciences) Melbourne Enterprise | Melbourne Research and Enterprise Portfolio Full-time | Fixed term for 5 years The University of Melbourne is home to some of Australia's most significant research, innovation and entrepreneurial capabilities, with a track record that includes strategic agreements with leading global organisations. This is a significant opportunity to build on that foundation, grow the University's external partnerships and unlock new opportunities across the health and life sciences sector. We are seeking an exceptional senior leader to lead Enterprise Partnerships across health and life sciences. Reporting to the Executive Director, Partnerships and Precincts, you will identify, shape and convert strategic opportunities that connect the University's world-class capabilities with industry, government, health services, medical research institutes and other key partners. As a senior institutional interface, you will bring together expertise from across the University to develop high-impact partnerships that accelerate research translation, support improved health outcomes, build workforce capability, influence policy and generate lasting economic and societal impact. The opportunity This is a career-defining opportunity to build on a strong foundation of global partnerships and shape the next generation of health and life sciences engagement at one of the world's leading universities. Building on a strong track record of strategic agreements that are among the first of their kind in Australia, you will have the opportunity to develop new partnerships and extend the University's impact across Australia and internationally. You will lead strategic partnership development, business development and key account management across the sector, building trusted relationships with senior external stakeholders and translating the University's research, education, talent and innovation capabilities into compelling partner propositions. You will also strengthen a more market-facing, sector-led and partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, while leading and developing a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. Key responsibilities include: Originate, shape and convert high-value strategic partnerships, collaborations and commercial opportunities across the health and life sciences sector. Build influential relationships with senior leaders across industry, government, healthcare, medical research and innovation, both nationally and internationally. Translate the University's research, education, talent, commercialisation and enterprise capabilities into compelling value propositions for partners. Identify and prioritise a strong pipeline of opportunities, applying commercial judgement, governance and disciplined account and performance management. Mobilise multidisciplinary expertise across faculties, research institutes and professional services to respond to complex opportunities and maximise impact. Strengthen a sector-led, partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, including data-informed decision-making and effective use of CRM and emerging technologies. Lead and develop a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. About you You are an accomplished partnerships, commercial, business development or enterprise leader with deep knowledge of the health and life sciences sector and a strong track record of creating and growing complex, high-value partnerships. You bring strategic insight, commercial acumen and exceptional relationship-building capability, with the credibility to engage senior stakeholders, navigate complex environments and turn opportunities into meaningful outcomes. You will bring: Significant experience leading strategic partnerships, business development or commercial activity within industry, government, healthcare, research or innovation environments. Deep knowledge of the health and life sciences sector, with the ability to identify emerging trends across biotechnology, medical technologies, pharmaceuticals, precision medicine, research translation and related fields and translate these into strategic partnership opportunities. Experience working within, or closely alongside, research-intensive, innovation-led or knowledge-based organisations, with the ability to connect complex research capability with external partner needs. A strong track record of establishing and growing influential relationships across industry, government, healthcare, research and innovation ecosystems and converting these into strategic opportunities. Demonstrated commercial judgement in structuring complex partnerships, evaluating opportunities, managing risk and navigating governance to achieve sustainable outcomes. Experience shaping new approaches, capabilities or operating models in complex environments and bringing diverse stakeholders with you. Outstanding communication, influencing and stakeholder engagement skills, with the judgement and credibility to build alignment and influence senior decision-makers. A collaborative and inclusive leadership style, with experience setting direction, building capability and developing high-performing teams. How to apply To apply, please submit: A current CV. A cover letter (maximum two pages) outlining your suitability for the role. For role or application process enquiries, please contact Lucy Porter , Executive Recruitment Partner, at exec-recruitment@unimelb.edu.au . If you are ready to build on world-class research and innovation capabilities, grow high-impact partnerships and shape lasting health, economic and societal impact, we invite you to apply. Applications must be submitted through the online application portal and will not be accepted by email. Please note: The University is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Applications close: Monday 9th November 2026 at 11:55pm Melbourne time.   Position Description: PD - Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health, LS).pdf PD - Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health, LS).docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Deputy-Director-of-Indigenous-Medical-Education_JR-018456-1": {
      "title": "Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full Time; Continuing Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department: Department of Medical Education Salary: Level B $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  Shape Indigenous medical education across Melbourne’s Doctor of Medicine program through curriculum innovation, meaningful partnerships and culturally safe practice to improve First Nations health outcomes. Apply now to drive strategic curriculum leadership, evidence-informed teaching and meaningful community engagement in one of Australia’s leading medical schools. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as these positions are exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic).  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   Help shape the next generation of doctors through leadership in Indigenous medical education. You will guide curriculum innovation, assessment and community engagement initiatives that advance culturally safe practice and contribute to improved First Nations health outcomes. What you will deliver:   Provide leadership for the development and delivery of First Nations health education across the Doctor of Medicine program, ensuring alignment with learning outcomes, curriculum priorities and accreditation requirements. Drive the design and continuous improvement of culturally safe, evidence-informed learning and assessment practices, including innovative teaching resources, placements and digital learning experiences. Support and enable high-performing teams and partnerships, fostering collaboration across academic, clinical and community settings to achieve impactful educational outcomes. Strengthen engagement with First Nations communities and health organisations, ensuring Indigenous knowledges, perspectives and priorities inform curriculum, educational practice and quality improvement activities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a tertiary qualification in health, education, public health or an equivalent combination of training. Bring experience in health professional education, with expertise in curriculum design, assessment and educational quality improvement in a tertiary or healthcare setting. Demonstrate leadership and collaboration skills, with the ability to influence practice, support teams and work effectively across complex organisational environments. Build strong and respectful relationships with diverse stakeholders, including First Nations communities, health services, academic colleagues and partner organisations. Have a strong understanding of First Nations health, cultural safety and health equity, together with a commitment to improving outcomes for First Nations peoples through education. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – “The Wurru Wurru Health Unit”   At the Wurru Wurru Health Unit, we are shaping the future of medical education. Through partnerships with First Nations communities, clinicians and educators we lead the design and delivery of First Nations health education that prepares future doctors to provide culturally safe, responsive and equitable care. As part of our team, you will have the opportunity to influence one of Australia's leading medical programs, contribute to sector-leading curriculum innovation, and work alongside passionate colleagues dedicated to advancing First Nations health and educational excellence. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the our recruitment process, including application tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A valid Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please submit: A current resume (CV) A cover letter outlining your interest in the role and relevant experience Your responses to the selection criteria, found in the position description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Dr Bridie Hofstee at b.hofstee@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process, contact Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments, contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 8 November 2026 11:55 PM; AEDT.  Position Description:   JR-018456 Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education PD.docx JR-018456 Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Associate-Professor-in-Periodontics_JR-009367-1": {
      "title": "Professor in Periodontics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.8 FTE) to Full-time; Continuing Faculty:  Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences School:  Melbourne Dental School Salary: Level D: AUD $183,859 - $202,556 p.a.              Level E: AUD $236,824 p.a.              plus 17% super and clinical loading of AUD $13,494 p.a. International leadership in specialist Periodontics / Shape a nationally benchmarked Doctor of Clinical Dentistry (DCD) Periodontics program / Drive impactful translational research Join us to lead specialist Periodontics education, research and clinical innovation that advances oral health globally. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead the Doctor of Clinical Dentistry (DCD) Periodontics program, integrating specialist clinical training, cutting-edge research and curriculum innovation. You will shape national and international standards in Periodontics while mentoring the next generation of specialist clinicians. What you will deliver:   Lead the design, delivery and evaluation of the DCD Periodontics curriculum and assessment across clinical and academic components. Provide high-quality clinical supervision and safe patient care at RDHM, modelling evidence-based, multidisciplinary specialist Periodontics practice. Establish and lead an internationally recognised research program in Periodontics, securing competitive funding and collaborative partnerships. Supervise and mentor higher degree and postgraduate research students, building research capacity across the discipline and School. Provide strategic leadership in national curriculum frameworks, accreditation standards and professional guidelines for specialist Periodontics. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold and maintain current or clearly eligible specialist AHPRA registration in Periodontics, without limiting conditions or restrictions. Possess a completed PhD in Periodontics or a closely related field, with strong academic credentials. Demonstrate strategic leadership in specialist clinical teaching, including curriculum design, assessment, benchmarking and educational innovation. Show a sustained record of high-quality research, including competitive grant success and impactful translational outcomes. Bring extensive experience supervising higher degree and postgraduate research students through to successful thesis completion. Engage confidently with national and international professional bodies, accreditation panels and multidisciplinary clinical partners. For further information please refer to the attached Candidate Information Pack .  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – “Melbourne Dental School”   You will join the Melbourne Dental School, a leader in specialist dental education and translational research. Working closely with the Royal Dental Hospital of Melbourne, you will help deliver world-class patient-centred clinical training. You will collaborate in a values-driven Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences, committed to Advancing Health 2030. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au   Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Professor Petros Papagerakis or Professor Ivan Darby at the details listed in the position description. For questions related to our recruitment process email Shabon Nawabi at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Friday 9th October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   Candidate Information Pack - Professor in Periodontics.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Senior-Lecturer--Economics---Econometrics_JR-018940-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer, Economics / Econometrics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time, Continuing Faculty: Faculty of Business and Economics Department/ School:  Department of Economics Salary: Level C - $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. plus 17% super  World-leading economics and econometrics department / Shape research, teaching and policy impact / Cooperative, well-resourced environment Join a top-ranked department to lead influential research, inspire students, and mentor future economists and econometricians. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. We strongly encourage applications from women and people who identify as women The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead high-quality research in economics or econometrics within a globally recognised, impact-driven department. You will teach engaged students, supervise ambitious researchers, and help shape innovative curricula and academic leadership. What you will deliver:   Produce internationally recognised research and actively secure external funding through grants, fellowships and collaborations. Deliver high-quality teaching in specialist and core subjects across undergraduate, honours and graduate programmes. Supervise and mentor honours, masters and PhD students, fostering rigorous research training and academic development. Provide academic leadership through subject coordination, curriculum innovation and contributions to departmental strategy and governance. Engage with government, industry and community partners to translate research insights into policy and practice. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in economics, econometrics or a closely related discipline, completed by commencement. Demonstrate a strong, sustained research record with publications in leading peer-reviewed economics or econometrics journals. Teach effectively in specialist economics or econometrics subjects and contribute to core undergraduate or postgraduate teaching. Secure, or show strong potential to secure, competitive research funding and cooperative policy or industry projects. Provide high-quality supervision and mentoring for honours, masters and PhD students across diverse research topics. Contribute positively to departmental leadership, service, curriculum development and external engagement with government or industry. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Department of Economics You will join the Faculty of Business and Economics, renowned for teaching with an impact, research and industry engagement. Within the Department of Economics, you will collaborate with leading economists, econometricians and actuaries across diverse fields. You will engage with specialised research units and labs, from applied microeconomics to market design and environmental data. Our collaborative, inclusive culture supports excellence, mentoring and strong connections with business, government and the wider community. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Phillip McCalman at econ-jobhire@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 15 January 2027 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   Word: JR-018940_Senior Lecturer in Economics and Econometrics_PD.docx PDF:   JR-018940_Senior Lecturer in Economics and Econometrics_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Casual-Tutors--Faculty-of-Business-and-Economics_JR-018769": {
      "title": "Casual Tutors, Faculty of Business and Economics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\n\nTutors are a critical part of teaching teams in the Faculty of Business and Economics. Under the guidance of a Subject Coordinator, Tutors will deliver weekly classes and contribute significantly to students' academic success and satisfaction with university life. The Faculty are seeking Tutors in the departments of Accounting, Economics, Finance, and Management & Marketing. Responsibilities include: - Preparing and delivering high quality, student-centered small group learning experiences - Providing student consultations - Marking assignments and exams About you: You have excellent written and verbal communication skills and a passion for teaching in accounting, economics, finance, management and/or marketing disciplines. You are able to navigate subject websites and teaching technology with ease and work collegially with others. You will also have: - An Honours or postgraduate level qualification in a relevant discipline and/or equivalent relevant academic or professional or practice-based experience and expertise - A current Victorian Working With Children Check (WWCC) by the commencement of the teaching period For further information please refer to the attached PD. Apply today! Applications MUST be submitted via the Casual Tutor Recruitment System (CTRS). Please visit https://ctrs.app.unimelb.edu.au/ctrs/ to apply. Applications submitted using the Apply Now button at the top of this advertisement will NOT   be reviewed. Applications Close: Sunday 8th of November at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description Word: 2027 FBE Tutor.docx PDF: 2027 FBE Tutor.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Early-Childhood-Educator-s---Teacher-s--Casual-Positions_JR-016427": {
      "title": "Early Childhood Educators & Teachers, Casual Positions",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-07\n\nPlease apply via this link: https://careers.giighire.com/universityofmelbourne/29027?source=link DO NOT APPLY VIA THE UOM CAREERS PAGE Role type: Casual positions Department : Student and Scholarly services Support high-quality early learning / collaborate in inclusive, research-informed practice / join a respected University childcare service Apply now to contribute your expertise in early childhood education across our vibrant University children’s centres. Investing in you – health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity You will support high-quality early childhood programs across our University centres, grounded in evidence-based pedagogy. You will collaborate with experienced colleagues to create inclusive, play-based learning that strengthens every child’s wellbeing. You will gain varied experience supporting different age groups, rooms, and services within a child-safe organisation.   What you will deliver: Plan, implement, and evaluate play-based learning programs aligned with EYLF, VEYLDF, and service philosophies. Document children’s learning, development, and wellbeing to inform individual planning and continuous programme improvement. Maintain safe, inclusive environments through active supervision, risk management, and adherence to all regulatory requirements. Build strong, respectful relationships with children, families, and colleagues to support belonging and collaborative practice. Collaborate with room leaders and teams to reflect on practice and support service quality improvement.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold an approved Early Childhood degree and current WWCC or VIT registration. Demonstrate sound knowledge of NQF, National Law and Regulations, and Victorian Child Safe Standards. Bring proven experience delivering high-quality education and care using EYLF, VEYLDF, and evidence-based pedagogies. Create inclusive, stimulating environments that promote children’s agency, participation, and strong sense of belonging. Apply diligent risk management, active supervision, and child-safe practices in dynamic early childhood settings. Adapt flexibly across rooms, age groups, and teams while maintaining professional, collaborative conduct.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Student and Scholarly Services Student and Scholarly Services supports students and staff from first enquiry through to graduation and beyond. Early Childhood Education Services operates three high-quality University children’s centres, delivering evidence-based programmes for infants to preschoolers. You will join a collaborative, research-informed team committed to child safety, inclusion, and continuous quality improvement.   Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants  We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials: Please ensure to apply through this link We will NOT be accepting applications through the UOM Careers Page You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience to the link provided as part of the application process. Please apply via this link: https://careers.giighire.com/universityofmelbourne/29027?source=link DO NOT APPLY VIA THE UOM CAREERS PAGE   Position Description: Early Childhood Educator’s & Teacher’s - Casual Positions.docx Early Childhood Educator’s & Teacher’s - Casual Positions.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Casual-Academics---Melbourne-Dental-School_JR-017697-1": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - Melbourne Dental School",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-21\nVariable time\n\nRole type:   Casual; Sessional academic   Faculty:   Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences   Department / School:   Melbourne Dental School   Salary:   Casual   sessional   academic rates as per University of Melbourne Enterprise Agreement, plus   1 2 % super     Shape the next generation of   oral health practitioners by   teaching    in   the   D octor of Dental Surgery (D DS ) ,   Bachelor of Oral Health ( BOH ) , and Doctor of Clinical Dentistry (DCD)   program s   at   Melbourne Dental   School   / Enjoy flexible, sessional academic work. Apply now to grow your clinical teaching   expertise   while guiding safe, evidence-based dental care for diverse patients       The University of Melbourne     We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to   benefit   society through the transformative impact of education and research.   Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity     You will play a key role in developing   dental and oral health therapy   students’ clinical competence and professional judgement.   You will supervise preclinical and clinical activities at   the   Melbourne Dental School , guiding safe, high-quality care for diverse patients.   You will also build your own teaching skills within a collaborative,   values -driven dental school community.     What you will deliver:     Prepare for preclinical and clinical teaching sessions, reviewing materials, patient   records   and   logistics   to support   evidence based   learning.   Supervise,   guide   and educate   students during preclinical and clinical sessions, focusing on skills acquisition and knowledge application.   Review and approve patient dental records and   clinic    documentation , ensuring   accurate , compliant clinical records for every session.   Facilitate student feedback discussions and complete required assessment, marking,   questionnaires   and sessional administrative documentation.   Participate in meetings,   training   and compliance activities to   maintain   credentialing, child   safety   and high-quality teaching standards.     You may be a great fit   if   you :     Hold a primary dental qualification, eligible for registration with the Dental Board of Australia.   Bring at least   two   years’ clinical experience   (varies between programs) , with strong, contemporary evidence-based dental practice skills.   Maintain current   Ahpra   registration with no conditions, and   clinic   credentialing   appropriate to   clinical teaching   on the MDS teaching clinics. Demonstrate strong motivation to develop excellence in dental education,   assessment   and student feedback.   Work effectively both independently and collaboratively, contributing positively to diverse students,   patients   and academic colleagues.   Exhibit well-developed communication and interpersonal skills, supporting students’ learning in complex preclinical and clinical environments.   For further   information   please refer to the attached   job duties   description .       Your new team –   Melbourne Dental School   At the Melbourne Dental School, you will join a values-driven community within the Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences. The   School   partners closely with the Royal Dental Hospital of Melbourne   (RDHM) , delivering high-impact clinical education and patient care. You will collaborate with skilled clinicians and educators who champion evidence-based practice,   innovation   and student success, while enjoying   strong support   for your professional development and wellbeing.     Be   y ourself     The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe   organisation . The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences.   Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're   committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities.   Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process .   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander   a pplicants     We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians.   Visit   our   Indigenous staff page   to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.           Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at   korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .     Application essentials:   Visit   \"apply with us\"   to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is   required   for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.  Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact   mds-operations@unimelb.edu.au     For questions related to our recruitment process email   hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at   hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').     Apply   today, and   join a community   that’s   shaping the future.   Job Duties Description – Doctor of Dental Surgery (DDS).pdf Job   Duties   Description – Bachelor of Oral Health   (BOH)   ( Coming   Soon)   Job   Duties   Description – Doctor of   Clinical   Dentistry   (DCD)   ( Coming   soon )"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Expressions-of-Interest--Indigenous-Employment-Opportunities-at-the-University-of-Melbourne_JR-006839": {
      "title": "Expressions of Interest: Indigenous Employment Opportunities at the University of Melbourne",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-04\nFull time\n\nThe University of Melbourne is inviting Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates to submit an Expression of Interest (EOI) for current and future opportunities across both professional and academic positions. Whether you are just starting your career or seeking the next step in your professional or academic journey, we encourage you to express your interest in joining our vibrant and diverse community. You may express interest in the following professional areas: Finance | Call Centre & Customer Service | Student Support Services | HR, Recruitment & Culture | Legal | Fundraising | Administration & Office Support | Management Roles | Strategy, Culture & Analytics | Information & Communications Technology | Marketing & Communications You may express interest in the following academic roles across all faculties: Tutor | Research Assistant | Research Fellow | Lecturer | Associate Professor Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as these positions are exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic).  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfillment, including:  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance, including Cultural Leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program with access to Indigenous counsellors for Indigenous staff Dedicated and ongoing support from Korin Korin, our Indigenous Employment and Development team A vibrant and active Indigenous Staff Network, providing opportunities for connection and professional development For more information check out our benefits page!  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. We believe the diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing reasonable adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. For assistance, please contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Contact details for other enquiries are located at the bottom of this page. Find more information for applicants requiring reasonable adjustments. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. outlines our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff and students.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin Korin, at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au for further information and support.  Our Indigenous strategy, Murmuk Djerring outlines our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff and students.   Please note : This role requires current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available.   A Working with Children Check (WWCC) is required for all positions. If successful, we'll guide you through the WWCC application process during onboarding and reimburse the cost. Join Us and unlock your career potential!   If this role is right for you, please apply with the following documents:  Resume   Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience Proof of Aboriginality Please note: As part of the application, you’ll be asked to share your preferences for the types of roles and levels of experience that best match your background. For recruitment queries, email Olivia korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Include the Position Number and Job Title in the subject line. Please do not send applications to this email address."
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Lecturer--Indigenous-Social-Equity-and-Leadership---Expression-of-Interest_JR-014395": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership - Expression of Interest",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-10\nFull time\n\nThe University of Melbourne is inviting Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates to submit an Expression of Interest (EOI) for a lecturer, Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership position on a full-time continuing basis. This opportunity is offered at Academic Level B, with a salary range of $124,656–$148,023 per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic).   Your next career opportunity:   You will design, teach and assess postgraduate subjects in Social Change Leadership for diverse experienced Fellows. You will advance research on Indigenous social change while supporting Fellows’ community-led projects and long-term leadership journeys. You will collaborate across the University and global Atlantic Fellows network to grow your academic and impact profile.  What you will deliver:   Design, deliver and coordinate engaging postgraduate subjects, using culturally grounded, flexible teaching approaches for adult learners.  Supervise and support Fellows to develop rigorous social change project proposals with strong community partnerships and ethical foundations.  Conduct and publish high-quality research on Indigenous social change and leadership, securing external funding where appropriate.  Contribute to AFSE’s research, seminars and public engagement, representing AFSE locally, nationally and internationally.  Build and sustain collaborations with Indigenous communities, partners and University colleagues to strengthen AFSE’s teaching and impact.    You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a doctoral qualification, or be near completion, in a field related to Indigenous social change.  Demonstrate experience co-designing curriculum that embeds Indigenous knowledges, cultural safety principles and decolonial teaching practices.  Show a strong record, or clear potential, in research, publication and collaborative project development.  Exhibit excellence in teaching adult learners, including experienced leaders, using inclusive and reflexive pedagogical approaches.  Work effectively within small, interdisciplinary teams, contributing constructively to programme development and shared strategic priorities.  Engage confidently with Indigenous communities and partners, nurturing ethical, long-term relationships that support social change.  Your new team:   Atlantic Fellows for Social Equity You will join the Atlantic Fellows for Social Equity, a collaborative programme advancing Indigenous and Pacific-led social change. AFSE combines a rich Foundation year of fellowship and postgraduate study while nurturing a lifelong community of senior Fellows. The team works closely with communities, partners and a global Atlantic Fellows network to amplify impact. You will collaborate with committed colleagues across the University, including the Faculty of Education, benefiting from strong mentoring and development opportunities.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences.  Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities.  Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our  Indigenous staff page  to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at  oied-hr@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit  \"apply with us\"  to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application. Want to know more?   For queries related to this expression of interest, please contact Program Director, Dr. Yvonne Ualesi at yvonne.ualesi@unimelb.edu.au      Position Description: PD_JR-014395 EOI Lecturer Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer-in-Visual-Arts-and-Design-Education_JR-019504": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Visual Arts and Design Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-06\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time (0.9 FTE); Fixed-term (14 months) Faculty: Education School: School of Curriculum and Pedagogy Salary: Level B: $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. (pro rata for part-time) plus 17% superannuation Education-focused academic role in Visual Arts and Design / coordinate flagship Master of Teaching Secondary Internship (MTSI) secondary learning area subjects / join a collaborative, research-informed Faculty of Education Apply now to shape future secondary Visual Arts and Design teachers and grow your education-focused academic career. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will coordinate and teach into core Visual Arts and Design learning area subjects in the Master of Teaching Secondary Internship. This includes designing contemporary curriculum and studio-based learning that connect disciplinary knowledge, pedagogy and school-based practice. Alongside this, you join an education-focused academic community committed to impactful and inclusive teacher education. What you will deliver:   Prepare and deliver high-quality Visual Arts and Design teaching across MTSI A-stream and other Master of Teaching cohorts. Design, review and maintain contemporary curriculum, assessment and studio-based, online and blended pedagogies in Visual Arts and Design Education. Teaching Visual Arts and Design subjects (Standard and Internship), including ePortfolio assessment moderation, marking, student communication and support for sessional teaching staff. Support pre-service teachers to connect theory, curriculum, pedagogy and professional practice through studio pedagogies and practices. Contribute to scholarship of teaching, engagement, leadership and service activities aligned with Faculty of Education priorities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD or EdD in Visual Arts or Design Education, or equivalent AQF Level 10 experience. Demonstrate strong teaching experience in secondary Visual Arts and/or Design education or Initial Teacher Education. Possess deep knowledge of contemporary curriculum, theory, pedagogy and policy in Visual Arts and Design Education. Manage and contribute effectively to Visual Arts and Design or Initial Teacher Education projects and initiatives. Coordinate subjects confidently, including assessment design, feedback, moderation and specialised support for pre-service teachers. Collaborate constructively within multidisciplinary teaching teams and work effectively with sessional teaching staff. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Faculty of Education   The Faculty of Education is a vibrant community transforming education practice locally and globally hosting studioFive. StudioFive is an award winning multi-and inter-disciplinary arts studio and UNESCO research ecology within the School of Curriculum and Pedagogy. The School of Curriculum and Pedagogy leads innovative teacher education, shaping curricula that address pressing social and educational issues. You will work with colleagues who value collaboration, practice-led teaching and learning, inclusive and just education, Indigenous scholarship and research-informed teaching. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Dr Kate Coleman, associate professor, visual arts and design education for details in PD. For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 19th November, 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019504 Lecturer in Visual Arts and Design Education_PD.docx JR-019504 Lecturer in Visual Arts and Design Education_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Assistant-in-Youth-Mental-Health_JR-019544": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Youth Mental Health",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-05\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time (1.0 FTE); Fixed term, 12 months  Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Department: Centre for Youth Mental Health  Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super      Shape national youth mental health service models / work across headspace Early Psychosis, YES and future YSCCs / collaborate with leading researchers at Orygen and the University of Melbourne  Apply now to contribute your research skills to improving complex youth mental health services nationwide  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.      The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    In this role, you will support national initiatives improving care for young people with complex mental ill-health.  You will contribute to fidelity research, data analysis and evaluation across headspace Early Psychosis, YES and future YSCCs.   What you will deliver:    Contribute to National Programs research projects, including ethics submissions, governance applications and small studies using existing datasets.  Coordinate REMIT fidelity processes across national headspace Early Psychosis sites, including visit logistics, data collection and reporting.  Co-develop and pilot a new transdiagnostic fidelity tool for upcoming Youth Specialist Care Centres with stakeholders.  Conduct qualitative and quantitative analyses, and contribute to publications, presentations and grey literature on youth mental health.  Engage with PHNs, services and colleagues through forums, meetings and collaborative tasks, fostering positive working relationships.  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a postgraduate degree in health, behavioural or social sciences, or are progressing towards a related PhD.  Demonstrate strong interest in youth mental health and motivation to build a research career in the sector.  Bring proven experience contributing to research projects, including data analysis, literature reviews or evaluation activities.  Apply excellent written and verbal communication skills to collaborate with diverse stakeholders and prepare high-quality outputs.  Manage multiple tasks effectively, negotiating priorities with supervisors and colleagues to meet project deadlines.  Use Microsoft Office confidently and, ideally, have experience with survey tools, REDCap, NVivo or similar platforms.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team – Centre for Youth Mental Health   You will join the Centre for Youth Mental Health within the Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences.  Working closely with Orygen’s National Programs, you will contribute to world-leading youth mental health research and innovation.  The team collaborates nationally with services, Primary Health Networks and clinicians, shaping evidence-based models of care.  You will be supported by a vibrant, multidisciplinary community committed to impact, equity and continuous improvement.   Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria   Want to know more?   For questions related to our recruitment process, email hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: 20th November 2026 at​ 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:     PD_Research Assistant in Youth Mental Health.docx   PD_Research Assistant in Youth Mental Health.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/NHMRC-Project-Officer_JR-019431": {
      "title": "NHMRC Project Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-04\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.2 FTE); Fixed term to 30 June 2027  Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Department/ School:  Melbourne Medical School  Salary: UOM 8: $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. (pro rata for part-time) plus 17% super   Strategic leadership of NHMRC-funded health services research / collaborate with clinicians, engineers and researchers / shape impactful healthcare innovation  Join Melbourne Medical School to coordinate complex research projects and ensure a landmark program delivers meaningful health outcomes.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    In this role, you will lead the operational delivery of a major NHMRC health services research program.  You will coordinate five interconnected projects, aligning multidisciplinary teams, budgets and milestones to meet ambitious research commitments.  You will work with significant autonomy, shaping decisions that influence healthcare practice and research translation.  What you will deliver:    Lead planning and delivery of five NHMRC health services projects, ensuring objectives, timelines and funding obligations are achieved.  Develop and maintain integrated program plans, schedules, milestones, dependencies, risk controls and reporting frameworks for governance.  Provide day-to-day leadership for research and operational staff, setting priorities, monitoring progress and resolving barriers to delivery.  Oversee program budgets and financial administration, advising on resource allocation, variances and material financial risks.  Build effective relationships with academics, clinicians, engineers and hospital partners, influencing decisions where formal authority is limited.    You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a degree in science, health, engineering or related discipline, combined with substantial relevant experience.  Demonstrate success managing complex research programs or multiple projects within university, health service or comparable environments.  Understand research methods and project lifecycles, with knowledge of implementation science or qualitative approaches highly regarded.  Bring experience coordinating multidisciplinary staff and influencing diverse stakeholders where direct reporting authority is limited.  Possess demonstrated capability in budget management, resource planning, milestone tracking, risk management and program reporting.  Excel in high-level judgement, problem solving and communication across academic, clinical, engineering and hospital-based partners.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team   At Melbourne Medical School, you will join a dynamic research environment focused on improving healthcare delivery and outcomes.  You will collaborate with leading academics, clinicians, engineers and hospital partners on health services research and implementation projects.  The team values collaboration, innovation and real-world impact, offering strong support for professional growth and autonomy.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Applicants should provide a brief response to each key selection criterion (dot points are preferred). Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria     Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Forbes McGain at forbes.mcgain@unimelb.edu.au  For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​15 November 2026​ 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    JR-019431 NHMRC Project Officer -PD.pdf   JR-019431 NHMRC Project Officer -PD.doc"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Manager--Sustainable-Healthcare_JR-019001-2": {
      "title": "Manager, Sustainable Healthcare",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-04\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.5–0.8 FTE); Fixed-term (12 months)  Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Salary: UOM 8; $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. plus 17% super    Faculty-wide sustainable healthcare leadership / connect research, teaching and hospitals / drive impactful climate and planetary health change   Faculty-wide sustainable healthcare leadership / connect research, teaching and hospitals / drive impactful climate and planetary health change   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.    The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    You will coordinate sustainable healthcare education, research and engagement across MDHS, shaping climate and planetary health practice.  You will work closely with leaders, students and partners to deliver projects with tangible health system sustainability outcomes.   What you will deliver:     Lead and coordinate sustainable healthcare education, research and engagement initiatives across all six MDHS Schools.   Manage planning, implementation and reporting for MDHS and MMS Sustainability, Climate and Health Strategies and projects.   Supervise student internships and small quality assurance or research projects, ensuring robust methodologies and meaningful outcomes.   Identify funding opportunities, contribute to grant and philanthropic applications, and support growth of sustainability teaching programs.   Represent sustainable healthcare priorities in Faculty forums, strengthening partnerships with hospitals, institutes and external stakeholders.     You may be a great fit if you:     Hold an undergraduate qualification in healthcare or environmental science; a relevant Masters is highly regarded.   Demonstrate strong project management capability, delivering complex, multi-stakeholder initiatives on time and within scope.   Apply rigorous quantitative and qualitative research methods to sustainability, climate change and healthcare policy or practice.   Engage confidently with academic, clinical and professional stakeholders, influencing outcomes without formal line authority.   Contribute to university teaching or curriculum, particularly in sustainability, planetary health or healthcare-related programmes.   Work independently in complex organisations, balancing competing priorities while maintaining high ethical and professional standards.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.    What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:    Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team   You will join the Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences, a leader in health and medical education. MDHS is rapidly expanding its sustainability, climate and planetary health work across six Schools and partner institutions.    Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .    Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.    A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.    Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Applicants should provide a brief response to each key selection criterion (dot points are preferred). Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria   Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Forbes McGain at forbes.mcgain@unimelb.edu.au   For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: ​15 November 2026​ 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:     JR-019001 Manager, Sustainable Healthcare - PD.docx   JR-019001 Manager, Sustainable Healthcare - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Project-Officer_JR-019709-3": {
      "title": "Project Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-04\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Fixed term (12 months)  Faculty:   Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Department/ School:  School of Biomedical Sciences – Department of Microbiology and Immunology  Salary: $100,847 - $109,163p.a. plus 17% super    Dynamic global health projects team / collaborate with world-leading infection and immunity experts / real impact on Asia-Pacific public health   Apply now to coordinate impactful pathogen genomics and public health programmes that strengthen surveillance and regional capacity.   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.    The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    In this Project Officer role, you will coordinate pathogen genomics and public health programmes across the Asia-Pacific.  You will work with international fellows and experts to deliver training, surveillance and capacity-building initiatives.  You will gain experience within a globally recognised institute at the forefront of infection and immunity.   What you will deliver:     Coordinate day-to-day activities for Centre for Pathogen Genomics and MDU PHL, including projects, meetings and events.   Manage communication and collaboration with multidisciplinary teams, partners and fellows locally and across the Asia-Pacific region.   Provide Secretariat support for working groups, including agendas, documentation, reporting, and coordination of remote and onsite events.   Support monitoring and reporting against strategic workplans, ensuring activities align with Centre and MDU PHL objectives.   Develop and deliver communications, including website content, social media and public-facing materials for global audiences.     You may be a great fit if you:     Hold a tertiary qualification in public health, global health, pathogen genomics or a closely related discipline.   Demonstrate experience managing complex projects, including planning, coordination, stakeholder engagement and progress monitoring.   Build and maintain strong relationships with diverse stakeholders across government, public health institutions, laboratories and academia.   Produce high-quality briefings, reports, proposals and public communications tailored to technical and non-technical audiences.   Use Microsoft Office and digital collaboration tools with high proficiency to support project documentation and reporting.   Bring motivation and initiative to global health or public health programmes, ideally with project management training.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.    What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:    Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team   You will join the Department of Microbiology and Immunology within the School of Biomedical Sciences. Based at the Peter Doherty Institute, the team integrates research, training and reference laboratory services.  You will work closely with the Microbiological Diagnostic Unit Public Health Laboratory and Centre for Pathogen Genomics. Together, these groups support regional surveillance, capacity building and innovation in infectious disease control across the Asia-Pacific.    Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.    You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application.      Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Lisa Carter at Lisa.carter@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: ​15 November 2026​ 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:     JR-019709 Project Officer- PD.docx   JR-019709 Project Officer- PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Commercial-Sites/Early-Childhood-Teacher_JR-019491": {
      "title": "Early Childhood Teacher",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-04\nPart time\n\nWe are seeking an outstanding Early Childhood Teacher to join our team in a unique, exceeding University Centre 3 days per week.  This part time teaching role (Monday, Tuesday, Friday each week) is available for the academic year of 2027, commencing January.  The right person will (for all, internal and external)   Be inspired by children’s thinking, and passionate about Arts Education, Indigenous Perspectives and Learning in Nature  undefined  Hold a deep understanding of Inquiry curriculum, and be skilled at planning, leading and documenting inquiry-based learning  undefined  Be a team person who is dedicated to contributing to the ongoing growth of a high-achieving Centre    Position Number: JR-019491  Role type:  Part Time (3 days per week: Monday, Tuesday and Friday); Fixed Term until December 2027   Location: Abbotsford   Department/ School: Student and Scholarly Services; Chief Operating Officer Portfolio   Salary: UoM 5 - $86,943 - $99,864 p.a. plus 17% super   Join our committed and experienced team as an Early Childhood Educator Teacher and contribute to providing a quality education in a leading early learning Centre.     Is this the perfect opportunity for you? Apply today ! Including the following documents:    Resume     Letter of Application outlining your interest and experience leading an inquiry curriculum grounded in:     Indigenous Perspectives     Learning in Nature     Arts Education    The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.    Your next career opportunity     You will design and deliver exemplary early childhood education programmes that align with national and Victorian frameworks. You will lead and mentor educators, partner closely with families, and support funded kindergarten delivery within a University-based service.   What you will deliver:     Design, implement and evaluate developmentally appropriate early childhood education programmes aligned with national and Victorian frameworks.   Lead, supervise and mentor educators in your team to ensure consistent, high-quality pedagogy and practice.   Observe, assess and document children’s learning, including developmental reports, parent–teacher interviews and transition statements.   Partner with families through clear, respectful communication about children’s progress, needs and learning experiences.   Ensure compliance with National Quality Standards, Education and Care legislation and Victorian Government kindergarten requirements.     You may be a great fit if you:   Hold an approved early childhood teaching degree meeting ACECQA, DET and National Regulations requirements.  Maintain current VIT registration, a Working with Children Check and HLTAID012 First Aid, including anaphylaxis and asthma.  Apply strong knowledge of National Quality Standards, Education and Care Regulations and child-safe standards.  Design and deliver play-based, inclusive and anti-bias early childhood programmes for children up to school entry.  Lead and support educator teams, providing guidance, feedback and reflective pedagogical leadership.  Communicate effectively with families and colleagues, using sound written, oral and basic information technology skills.    For further information, please refer to the attached PD.      What we offer you!     We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:    Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page!      Your new team – Student and Scholarly Services     You will join Student and Scholarly Services within the Chief Operating Officer Portfolio, supporting the University’s youngest learners and their families. Early Childhood Education Services operates three centres that deliver high-quality education and care grounded in contemporary early childhood theory and practice. You will work with dedicated educators who value collaboration, inclusion and continuous improvement, and who are committed to exceeding National Quality Standards while supporting the broader University community.     Be Yourself     The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.    We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.      Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants     We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin Korin, our Indigenous Employment and Development team, via korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au      Application essentials:    Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.    A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload a separate document addressing the Selection Criteria outlined in the Position Description.     Want to know more?    For queries related to this specific position, contact Helen Thomson at helen.thomson@unimelb.edu.au     For questions related to our recruitment process email Tien Nguyen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close:  Wednesday, November 18th 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:    PD_JR-019491 Early Childhood Teacher.docx   PD_JR-019491 Early Childhood Teacher.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Senior-Research-Finance-Partnering-Lead_JR-016946-1": {
      "title": "Senior Research Finance Partnering Lead",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-04\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full Time; Continuing  Department:  Research, Innovation and Commercialisation (RIC)  Salary:  UoM 9 - $146,049 - $151,951 plus 17% super  Senior leadership in research finance / shape shared services innovation / enable world-class research impact  Apply now to lead research finance partnering and support transformative, externally funded projects across the University.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.     The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research.  Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    In this senior partnering role, you will lead research finance operations for a major Faculty cluster. You will guide researchers through complex funding rules while driving innovation in shared services processes and systems.  What you will deliver:    Maintain trusted advisory relationships with senior researchers and Faculty leaders on budgeting, forecasting and research financial management.  Lead and coach Research Finance Partnering staff, fostering high performance, continuous improvement and strong client service.  Coordinate day-to-day research finance operations, optimising resource allocation, processes, and enabling technologies across the team.  Provide specialist faculty-level research finance advice, including reporting, forecasting and budgeting for complex project portfolios.  Contribute to continuous improvement of people, processes, systems and performance within the finance shared services model.  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a relevant business degree plus CA, CPA or equivalent professional accounting qualification.  Bring extensive experience managing research funding from multiple agencies, ensuring compliance and sector best practice.  Lead professional finance teams, applying contemporary leadership, coaching and performance management to build capability.  Demonstrate strong financial modelling skills and the ability to interpret complex data for senior stakeholders.  Adapt effectively within complex matrix structures, championing business improvement initiatives and continuous process enhancement.  Manage diverse stakeholders with excellent organisational skills, prioritising competing deadlines while delivering responsive client service.  For further information, please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team   Research Finance sits within Research, Innovation and Commercialisation, enabling the University’s research and innovation mission. We provide end-to-end financial support across thousands of externally funded projects, ensuring compliance and transparent reporting to funders. Our team collaborates closely with academic leaders, business development experts and operational finance colleagues across all divisions. We value service excellence, big-picture thinking and attention to detail, creating an innovative, supportive environment for professionals.  Joining us, you contribute directly to world-class research impact while growing your leadership and technical expertise.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences.  Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities.  Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our  Indigenous staff page  to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.   Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin Korin, our Indigenous Employment and Development team, via   korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au   Application essentials:   Visit  \"apply with us\"  to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your  resume and a cover letter  outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.  Please upload a  separate document addressing the Selection Criteria  outlined in the Position Description.    Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Connie Killey via email at  connie.killey@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at  hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au  (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: Wednesday 18 November 2026 at 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:   JR-016946 Senior Research Finance Partnering Lead -PD.docx JR-016946 Senior Research Finance Partnering Lead -PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow-in-Numerical-Cosmology_JR-019578": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Numerical Cosmology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-02\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-Term for 2 years  Faculty: Faculty of Science  Department/ School: School of Physics  Salary: Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super (PhD entry level $110,319) Innovative research in inhomogeneous cosmology / work with state-of-the-art numerical relativity simulations or observational data / collaborate within a vibrant astrophysics community. Advance numerical cosmology, strengthen your research profile and contribute to the development of future cosmological surveys. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will join a leading astrophysics group to undertake research in inhomogeneous cosmology from a numerical, theoretical, or observational perspective. You will work with advanced general relativistic simulations, develop observational tests of standard cosmology, or contribute to our theoretical understanding of the inhomogeneous Universe. You will develop your own research direction while collaborating with colleagues across the Astrophysics Group and School. What you will deliver:   Conduct internationally competitive research in the field of general-relativistic inhomogeneous cosmology. Publish high-quality papers, present at seminars and conferences, and disseminate findings to academic and public audiences. Contribute to research funding proposals and engage with professional or industry partners to foster collaborative projects. Support training and co-supervision of undergraduate research students and mentor junior researchers in your expertise area. Contribute to School activities, committees, outreach and diversity initiatives, while upholding University values and compliance. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a completed or near-completed PhD in physics, astrophysics or a closely related discipline. Demonstrate experience in numerical cosmology or general relativity (broadly defined, e.g. simulations or numerical data analysis). Bring a sound publication record, and proven ability to prepare high-quality manuscripts and research reports. Possess proficiency with high-performance computing, simulated data handling, and languages such as Python, Fortran or C++. Manage research projects with limited supervision, showing excellent organisation, teamwork and ability to meet deadlines. Value building an academic research career, engaging collaboratively across diverse teams and contributing to inclusive environments. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – School of Physics   The Astrophysics Group sits within the School of Physics, a leading Australian centre for physics research. You will join a diverse community of academics, researchers and students working across multiple frontier physics areas. The School hosts ARC Centres of Excellence and major national facilities, offering rich opportunities for collaboration and impact. Within the Faculty of Science, you will benefit from world-class infrastructure, strong interdisciplinary links and inclusive culture. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .  Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.  Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.  Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Dr. Hayley Machperson at hayley.macpherson@unimelb.edu.au   For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').  Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 2 December 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019578 - Research Fellow in Numerical Cosmology - PD.docx JR-019578 - Research Fellow in Numerical Cosmology - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Clinical-Sites-Metro--Regional/Clinical-Research-Ethics-Specialist---Admin_JR-019687": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Ethics Specialist & Admin",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-02\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Fixed-term (1 year) Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/ School:  Department of Medicine, St Vincent’s Hospital Salary: UOM 6 - $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% super  Work on first-in-human diabetes technology trials / shape high-impact ethics and governance / grow specialist clinical research expertise Apply now to use your ethics expertise in research that transforms care for people with diabetes. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead ethics and governance submissions that enable pioneering diabetes clinical trials at St Vincent’s Hospital. You will join a passionate research group where your administration skills directly support life-changing technologies. What you will deliver:   Prepare high quality HREC and governance submissions from feasibility to approval for Diabetes Technology Research Group trials. Develop protocols, consent forms and Australian research application documentation, and coordinate clinical trial contracts and notifications. Maintain clinical trial management systems, documentation and financial processes, ensuring compliance with GCP and SOPs. Provide administrative support for clinical trials, including study payment activities and study equipment and supply purchase and distribution to participating sites Provide administrative support across University and hospital sites, including meetings, minutes, stakeholder correspondence and document preparation. Contribute to training new staff, promoting ethical practice, data confidentiality and collaborative relationships across the research group.   You may be a great fit if you:   Bring at least twelve months’ experience coordinating or reviewing HREC applications and governance in clinical research. Understand Australian clinical trial activation requirements for medical device studies with local and international collaborators. Apply extensive knowledge of ICH GCP guidelines, including ICH E6(R3), to evolving research practice. Use ERMs and clinical trial management systems confidently to manage documentation, workflows and regulatory correspondence. Demonstrate strong organisation, attention to detail and time management, meeting complex deadlines with minimal supervision. Hold a relevant tertiary qualification in Health or Life Sciences and experience in hospital or research settings. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – You will join the Department of Medicine at St Vincent’s Hospital within the Melbourne Medical School. Here, clinicians and researchers collaborate on leading edge diabetes technology trials that aim to transform patient outcomes. You will be part of the Diabetes Technology Research Group, a supportive team passionate about ethical, impactful research. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Lei by emailing laul@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 16 November 2026. 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019687 Clinical Research Ethics Specialist & Admin PD.docx JR-019687 Clinical Research Ethics Specialist & Admin PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Bioinformatician---International-training-and-research_JR-019680-3": {
      "title": "Bioinformatician – International training and research",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-02\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Fixed-term (12 months) Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/ School:  Department of Microbiology and Immunology, School of Biomedical Sciences Salary: $124,656 – $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  Deliver regional bioinformatics capacity building / drive genomics-informed infectious disease surveillance / collaborate with global public health partners Apply now to shape pathogen genomics capacity and drive impactful public health responses across the region. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   In this role, you will develop and deliver bioinformatics training that underpins genomic surveillance across the Asia-Pacific. You will collaborate with global partners to deploy analysis pipelines and translate genomics into public health action. You will also contribute to high-impact research and shape policy and practice in pathogen genomics. What you will deliver:   Design and deliver bioinformatics training and capacity building programmes for regional public health genomics teams. Implement, maintain and optimise analysis pipelines and platforms supporting pathogen genomics in public health laboratories. Contribute to translational research, funding applications and publications in microbial genomics and genomic epidemiology. Build and nurture communities of practice, mentoring students, postdoctoral researchers and regional partners. Represent the Centre in national and international forums, informing policy and best practice in genomic surveillance.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD, Masters or equivalent in bioinformatics, computational biology or a closely related discipline. Bring at least five years' bioinformatics and genomics experience, ideally in microbiology or infectious diseases. Demonstrate proficiency installing, managing and troubleshooting bioinformatics pipelines, tools and data storage or compute environments. Communicate complex genomic analyses clearly through high-quality data visualisation, publications and presentations. Possess experience delivering training or teaching in bioinformatics or genomics for diverse scientific and public health audiences. Show willingness to travel, collaborate internationally and contribute to capacity building in low-resource settings. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team You will join the Department of Microbiology and Immunology within the School of Biomedical Sciences. Based at the Doherty Institute, the Centre for Pathogen Genomics unites experts in genomics, epidemiology and public health. You will work in a collaborative, values-driven environment that advances human health through cutting-edge research, training and translation. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Torsten Seemann at t.seemann@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 15 November 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019680 Bioinformatician – International training and research-PD .pdf JR-019680 Bioinformatician – International training and research-PD .docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Clinical-Sites-Metro--Regional/Research-Fellow-Senior-Research-Fellow_JR-018991": {
      "title": "Research Fellow/Senior Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-02\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part Time (0.4FTE); Fixed Term until June 2029 Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/ School:  Department of Medicine; Melbourne Medical School Salary: Level B: $124,656 - $148,023 or Level C: $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. pro rata for part-time plus 17% super  Dedicated chronic disease research fellowship / deepen UniMelb–Austin Health collaboration / pathway to independent funding and clinical research leadership  Apply now to lead impactful chronic disease research, mentor emerging clinicians and scientists, and shape partnerships across Austin Health.   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will advance high-impact chronic disease research that directly connects university innovation with Austin Health clinical practice. You will develop your independent research profile through leadership, funding success, supervision and multidisciplinary, multi-site collaborations. What you will deliver:   Lead or co-lead innovative chronic disease research projects in collaboration with Austin Health and University colleagues. Secure competitive research funding as chief or co-investigator, contributing to strategic, team-based grant applications. Produce high-quality publications and conference outputs, including lead or senior authorship in peer-reviewed journals. Supervise and mentor research higher degree candidates and medical students, fostering excellent research training experiences. Build and sustain networks with clinical, industry, community and Indigenous partners to deepen translational research impact.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD or DMedSci in a biomedical, clinical or health field relevant to chronic disease. Maintain a clinical or research appointment based at Austin Hospital or Heidelberg Repatriation Hospital in a relevant discipline. Demonstrate a growing national profile in chronic disease research, evidenced by competitive funding and peer-reviewed publications. Exhibit strong judgement, initiative and the ability to coordinate complex, multidisciplinary research activities and teams. Provide effective supervision and mentoring for research higher degree candidates and medical students in clinical research settings. Manage administrative responsibilities, including committees, reporting and project governance, with excellent written and oral communication. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – You will join the Department of Medicine, partnering closely with Austin Health to tackle complex chronic diseases. Our team integrates clinicians, scientists and trainees, translating discoveries into better treatments and outcomes for diverse communities. We champion collaboration, compassion, respect, integrity and accountability, and support your development as an independent research leader. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Sharon by emailing dom-head@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 10 November. 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018991 Research Fellow, Senior Research Fellow PD.docx JR-018991 Research Fellow, Senior Research Fellow PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Early-Childhood-Educator--Diploma-_JR-017245": {
      "title": "Early Childhood Educator (Diploma)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-02\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full time and Part Time; Continuing and Fixed Term roles available Department: Student and Scholarly Services Salary: UOM 4 - $80,400 - $85,328 p.a. plus 17% super University-based early childhood service / Lead high-quality programs aligned with national frameworks / Support children, families and colleagues Apply now to shape quality early learning, mentor educators, and contribute to continuous centre improvement. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity In this Diploma-qualified Educator role, you will design and deliver high-quality programs for children. You will support children aged three months to five years across inclusive, responsive learning environments. You will also contribute to centre improvement, mentor educators, and strengthen relationships with families and colleagues.   What you will deliver: Design and implement inclusive, play-based programs aligned with the EYLF and National Quality Framework. Observe, document and assess children’s learning, using professional records to inform planning and evaluation. Create safe, engaging learning environments, supervising children and meeting their wellbeing and care needs. Collaborate with educators and leaders to reflect on pedagogy and improve teaching practice and outcomes. Build respectful, inclusive relationships with families and colleagues, sharing information and advising centre management. You may be a great fit if you: Hold an ACECQA-approved Diploma qualification and a current Employee Working with Children Check. Bring at least two years’ experience in early childhood education within regulated centre-based services. Demonstrate ability to design, implement and evaluate programs aligned with EYLF and National Quality Standards. Supervise and mentor educators or students, identifying strengths and improvement areas within your room. Apply detailed knowledge of relevant early childhood legislation, frameworks, child safety standards and food safety requirements. Use strong IT and communication skills to maintain documentation and collaborate effectively with families and colleagues.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Early Childhood Education Services Early Childhood Education Services sits within Student and Scholarly Services and delivers university-affiliated early learning across three centres. At Queensberry Children’s Centre, you will join a dedicated team providing high-quality education and care for children from three months to five years. The team values collaboration, reflective practice and inclusive relationships with families, offering you strong professional support and opportunities to contribute to continuous service improvement.   Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at oied-hr@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Tien at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: Monday, 16th November 2026 at 11:55 PM; AEST. Position Description: JR-017514 - Early Childhood Educator, Diploma - PD.docx JR-017514 - Early Childhood Educator, Diploma - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Casual-Tutor---Studio-Leader---Faculty-of-Architecture--Building-and-Planning_JR-020169-1": {
      "title": "Casual Tutor / Studio Leader - Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-06\n\nThe Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning are seeking suitably qualified sessional academics for Tutor and Studio Leader positions. Responsibilities for Tutors include: - Conducting tutorials in the required mode (e.g online, classroom, blended) to a high standard - Consulting with students in relation to readings, weekly topic, assessment and skills development - Undertaking marking and providing timely feedback to students Responsibilities for Studio Leaders include: - Facilitating the completion of studio tasks - Assessing student work in accordance with assessment framework - Providing students with high quality and details assessment feedback About you: You have excellent communication skills and a passion for teaching in within the disciplines of Architecture, Building and/or Planning. You are able to navigate subject websites and teaching technology with ease and work collegially with others. You will also have: - Tutors: Relevant Bachelor's degree and Honors/Graduate Diploma or Master's level post-graduate qualification - Studio Leader: Industry expertise in a relevant discipline and undergraduate and graduate degrees in Architecture, Landscape Architecture, Urban Design / other associated discipline - A current Victorian Working With Children Check (WWCC) by the commencement of the teaching period For further information please refer to the attached PD. Apply today! Applications MUST be submitted via the Casual Tutor Recruitment System (CTRS). Please visit https://ctrs.app.unimelb.edu.au/ctrs/ to apply. Applications submitted using the Apply Now button at the top of this advertisement will NOT   be reviewed. Applications Close: Wednesday 2nd of December at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: Sessional Studio Leader: ABP PD Sessional Studio Leader.docx Sessional Tutor: ABP PD Sessional Tutor.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Visitor-Experience-Lead_JR-018460-1": {
      "title": "Visitor Experience Lead",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-06\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing Department: Museums and Collections Salary: UOM 6 - $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% super  Shape visitor experiences across innovative university museums and galleries / lead diverse front-of-house teams / champion training and development  Join us to deliver safe, welcoming and inclusive museum and gallery visits for students, staff and general public  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   In this role, you will lead front-of-house teams across multiple museums and gallery venues. You will shape inclusive, engaging visitor experiences while ensuring safe, smooth operations every day. Working closely with curatorial, programs and operations colleagues, you will influence how audiences connect with exhibitions.  What you will deliver:   Lead, mentor and supervise casual Visitor Experience teams to maintain clear expectations, accountability and wellbeing.  Plan and deliver recruitment, induction and exhibition training programmes that align with curatorial, operational and service standards.  Coordinate day-to-day visitor operations across sites, resolving issues and incidents with timely communication and escalation.  Implement visitor experience policies, reporting and feedback systems to support continuous improvement across venues.  Contribute audience-focused insights to exhibition and programme planning, ensuring accessibility and engagement priorities are embedded.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a relevant degree or equivalent experience in arts, cultural management, visitor services or related disciplines.  Bring strong experience coordinating visitor or customer operations in complex, multi-venue or high-volume public environments.  Demonstrate success supervising and coaching large, diverse casual teams, including setting expectations and supporting performance.  Design and facilitate engaging training, induction and development activities for staff or volunteers.  Apply sound knowledge of risk, occupational health and safety, accessibility and visitor experience in public venues.  Use operational data, reporting and incident insights to improve workflows, systems and audience experiences.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!      Your new team:   You will join Museums and Collections, part of Chancellery’s Global Culture and Engagement portfolio. The department stewards Buxton Contemporary, Science Gallery Melbourne, Potter Museum of Art, Grainger Museum and other cultural sites. Together, teams collaborate to connect students, staff and communities with collections, creativity, research and diverse cultural perspectives.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter addressing the Selection Criteria outlined in the Position Description (no more than two pages). Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria     Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Erin Milne at erin.milne@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​ Sunday 15 November 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:   PD_JR-018460 Visitor Experience Lead.docx  PD_JR-018460 Visitor Experience Lead.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Director---Engagement--Defence-Science-Insitute_JR-019013-1": {
      "title": "Director - Engagement, Defence Science Institute",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-06\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time, Fixed Term to September 2028 Faculty: Engineering and Information Technology Department/School:  Computing and Information Systems Salary: Level D - $183,859 - $202,556 p.a. plus 17% super  Strategic leadership role / Shape Defence research and innovation across Victoria and Tasmania / High-profile engagement with senior stakeholders Join DSI to lead collaborative Defence R&D, connecting universities, industry and government to deliver national impact. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead DSI’s engagement and operations, shaping Defence innovation across Victoria and Tasmania. You will connect universities, industry and government, translating research into capability and driving multi-year strategic initiatives. What you will deliver:   Lead DSI’s engagement strategy and day-to-day operations in partnership with the Director – Academic and Associate Director. Design, deliver and refine funding programs that grow Defence-relevant R&D and talent pipelines across partner universities. Maintain up-to-date intelligence on Defence-focused research capability, matching universities and industry to national priorities and opportunities. Cultivate senior relationships with governments, Defence and industry, representing DSI at missions, delegations and key events. Secure and steward long-term funding, manage budgets, track KPIs and report to DSI’s governance bodies. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD or equivalent experience in a Defence-relevant discipline or closely aligned industry sector. Demonstrate a track record leading multi-year, large-scale projects within universities, Defence organisations or complex partnerships. Excel at building trusted relationships with senior stakeholders across government, industry and university leadership, nationally and internationally. Show success in securing or managing major funding for research or research-facilitation programs and initiatives. Possess Australian citizenship and the ability to obtain and maintain an NV1 security clearance and WWCC. Display deep understanding of Defence innovation ecosystems, strategies and priorities, or readiness to rapidly develop this expertise. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Defence Science Institute You will join the Defence Science Institute, a joint University–Defence–Government centre hosted within FEIT. DSI links ten universities, Defence and industry to grow research, innovation, skills and jobs in Victoria and Tasmania. As part of the national ADSUN network, the team enables cross-State initiatives in Defence innovation, science and technology. You will work in a collaborative, mission-focused environment that values diversity, partnership and real-world impact. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application . Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Toby Murray at toby.murray@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 6 December 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019013 - DSI Director - Engagement Position Description.pdf JR-019013 DSI Director - Engagement Position Description (Accessible).docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Brand-and-Digital-Marketing-Manager_JR-019547": {
      "title": "Brand and Digital Marketing Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-06\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (12-month maternity leave cover)  Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Department/School: Peter Doherty Institute for Infection and Immunity  Salary: UOM 8 - $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. plus 17% super    Strategic brand leadership in a renowned infection and immunity institute / shape high-impact digital channels with data-led insights / collaborate with expert clinicians, researchers and communicators  Apply now to lead Doherty Institute’s brand and digital presence, connecting diverse audiences with life-saving science.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.        The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    In this role you will steward the Doherty Institute brand across all digital channels and touchpoints.  You will drive data-informed strategies, optimise platforms and deliver engaging content that supports infectious disease research and impact.   What you will deliver:    Lead brand stewardship, ensuring consistent application of core brand elements across traditional and digital touchpoints.  Oversee website and intranet performance, usability and content governance, coordinating updates to meet audience needs.  Design and implement social media and EDM campaigns that build, nurture and engage priority audiences.  Analyse digital channel performance, report insights and recommend improvements to optimise reach, engagement and reputation.  Provide expert advice to senior leaders and jointly supervise communications support, fostering collaborative stakeholder relationships.  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a relevant marketing or communications degree, diploma or equivalent experience in complex organisations.  Demonstrate advanced expertise across digital channels, analytics, CMS, CRM and tools like Salesforce, WordPress and Canva.  Bring a strong track record delivering end-to-end digital marketing campaigns and projects in dynamic environments.  Produce targeted, engaging copy and visual content tailored to diverse audiences and scientific subject matter.  Exercise sound judgement in risk management, reputational issues and complex decision making with minimal supervision.  Engage stakeholders across academia, healthcare and partner organisations, building productive relationships and collaborative outcomes.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team – Doherty Institute   You will join the Doherty Institute, a world-leading partnership advancing infectious disease research and global health.  The Communications Team is collaborative, expert and committed to translating complex science into accessible, impactful stories.  You will partner with clinicians, researchers and professional staff across the University and hospital, shaping the Institute’s reputation.   Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Want to know more?   For questions related to our recruitment process email at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​20 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:     PD_Brand and Digital Marketing Manager_Doherty Institute.docx PD_Brand and Digital Marketing Manager_Doherty Institute.pdf"
