    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Support-Coordinator_JR-019927": {
      "title": "Research Support Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-09\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.4 FTE); Fixed term until 31 December 2027 Faculty: Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/ School:  Melbourne Medical School Salary: $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. (pro rata for part time) plus 17% super Engage with a diverse range of health and medical researchers and develop an understanding of the broader research ecosystem. Apply now to shape research support across Australia’s leading medical school and grow your research administration expertise. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   In this role, you will coordinate research support initiatives across a world-leading medical school. You will help promote seminars, grants and other opportunities to strengthen research quality and training for staff and students. You will gain broad exposure to diverse medical research disciplines and the wider University research ecosystem. What you will deliver:   Coordinate promotion of research and research training activities, including web content, e-newsletters and recognition of achievements. Plan and implement seminars, symposia, workshops and initiatives that enhance research quality and outputs across the School. Facilitate access to Faculty and University research support programs, connecting staff and students with relevant opportunities. Provide secretariat support for key committees and meetings, including agendas, papers, minutes and follow-up actions. Deliver executive support to the Research Director and contribute to strategic School-level projects and events. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a relevant degree or bring equivalent experience in complex administrative or research support environments. Demonstrate strong time management, planning and organisational skills, with proven ability to balance competing deadlines. Communicate effectively with diverse stakeholders and produce clear, engaging written materials, including newsletters and web content. Exercise sound judgement, initiative and discretion, handling confidential information and problem-solving with a continuous improvement mindset. Bring experience providing secretariat support, including agendas, minutes, membership management and tracking meeting action items. Use Microsoft Office and related systems confidently, with strong attention to detail and openness to new technologies. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation. Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being. Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning. Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company. Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program. For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Melbourne Medical School Melbourne Medical School is Australia’s oldest medical school and a global leader in teaching, research and policy. Based in the Melbourne Biomedical Precinct, the School partners with leading hospitals and research institutes across Victoria. As part of the Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences, you will join a collaborative, impact-focused community. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact David Lane by emailing david.lane@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 17 November 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019927 Research Support Coordinator PD.docx JR-019927 Research Support Coordinator PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Dookie-Victoria/Research-Assistant-in-Crop-Agronomy_JR-019846": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Crop Agronomy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-09\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-Term for 3 years  Faculty: Faculty of Science  Department/ School: School of Agriculture, Food and Ecosystem Sciences  Salary:  UOM4: $80,400 - $85,328 p.a. plus 17% superannuation  Hands-on agronomy research across field, glasshouse and laboratory settings / collaborate with leading crop scientists and growers / contribute to productivity gains across Australia's grains industry.  Join our Crop Agronomy Group and contribute to impactful research improving dryland grain cropping systems.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.       The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity   In this role, based at the University of Melbourne's Dookie campus, you will support high-quality agronomy experiments across field, glasshouse and laboratory environments.  You will collaborate with experienced scientists, farmers and industry partners to improve winter crop productivity and profitability.  You will develop broad technical skills in experiment management, machinery operation, sampling workflows and agronomic data collection.  What you will deliver:    Assist with designing, establishing and maintaining field, glasshouse and laboratory crop experiments under general direction.  Conduct sampling, measurements and basic analyses, ensuring accurate data collation, management and documentation for research projects.  Maintain workflows, consumables, equipment and agricultural machinery, promoting safe, efficient and reliable research operations.  Monitor experiment progress, identify emerging issues, and escalate concerns promptly to project leads and senior staff.  Provide task instructions to casual staff and students, supporting safe equipment use and achievement of project objectives.  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold or are near completion of a bachelor’s degree in agriculture, agricultural science or related discipline.  Demonstrate experience managing and measuring agricultural field experiments with limited supervision, delivering high-quality technical support.  Prioritise competing tasks effectively, managing time, resources and teams of casual staff to meet project goals.  Engage confidently with farmers, industry bodies and research partners to foster productive, collaborative relationships.  Maintain physical capacity for repetitive manual handling and outdoor fieldwork in varied and sometimes adverse weather.  Possess a current Australian driver’s licence, with desirable experience using farm machinery and agricultural chemicals safely.  For further information please refer to the attached Position Description.   What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program     For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – School of Agriculture, Food and Ecosystem Sciences   Your new team is the Crop Agronomy Group within the School of Agriculture, Food and Ecosystem Sciences. Together, you will tackle real-world challenges in dryland grain production systems across Australia.  You will join a vibrant, interdisciplinary community that connects biophysical, social and technical sciences to deliver impact.  Based at the Dookie Campus, you will enjoy unique field facilities, close industry partnerships and hands-on learning environments.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria     Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Dr. Corinne Celestina at corinne.celestina@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').     Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: 29 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone (Australian Eastern Daylight Time).   Position Description:    JR-019846 - Research Assistant in Crop Agronomy - PD.docx   JR-019846 - Research Assistant in Crop Agronomy - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/MathQuEST-Postdoctoral-Researcher-on-Quantum-Optimisation_JR-020003": {
      "title": "MathQuEST Postdoctoral Researcher on Quantum Optimisation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-09\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time or part-time; Fixed-term (4 years) Faculty: Science School: School of Mathematics and Statistics Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. (PhD entry level $110,319 p.a.) plus 17% super (retirement savings system/pension scheme) Build improved approaches to quantum algorithms for combinatorial optimisation / a brand-new ARC Centre of Excellence uniting 9 Australian universities and global partners / build an independent academic trajectory Apply now to advance quantum optimisation in a globally connected ARC Centre and establish your own academic direction. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity You will drive cutting-edge research on quantum algorithms for challenging combinatorial optimisation problems within the ARC Centre of Excellence in Mathematics for Quantum Era Security & Trust (MathQuEST) . Collaborating with MathQuEST researchers and partners, and Melbourne’s quantum ecosystem, develop an independent research profile while mentoring students and engaging industry partners. MathQuEST Postdoctoral Researcher on Quantum Optimisation Postdoctoral position at the University of Melbourne to undertake research on quantum optimisation as part of the ARC Centre of Excellence in Mathematics for Quantum Era Security & Trust (MathQuEST), under the guidance of Dr Lindon Roberts and Prof Kate Smith-Miles in collaboration with Chief Investigators across the Centre. MathQuEST is an Australian Research Council Centre of Excellence led by Professor Nalini Joshi. Funded over seven years, the Centre brings nine collaborating Australian universities: The University of Sydney, Adelaide University, University of Melbourne, Monash University, Queensland University of Technology, University of Queensland, University of Technology Sydney, University of Western Australia, and University of Wollongong. The Centre’s partner network comprises world-leading companies, universities, government, and professional bodies across 10 countries. A full list of partners is available on our website: https://mathquest.org.au/ . The MathQuEST vision is to undertake transformational interdisciplinary research founded on mathematics that will underpin new algorithms to protect our future, empower discovery, and increase trust in an age that will be shaped by quantum computers. Its core mission is to do so by creating a high-quality, inclusive and collaborative research culture to grow a mathematically trained, technologically agile workforce that will support Australia’s sovereign capability. All members of the Centre will have the opportunity to develop communication skills, participate in outreach, and receive mentoring and training to engage with society and industry at all levels. The Centre will be grounded in a culture of inclusion, diversity, and engagement of talented people at all levels, to cultivate discovery. MathQuEST supports a flexible, family-friendly working environment. Subject to visa restrictions, this opportunity is available as either a full-time or part-time position. This position, based at the University of Melbourne in Melbourne, is for 4 years and is aimed at build improved approaches to quantum algorithms in a broad sense, investigating both modelling and algorithmic advances. For more information on how to apply for this position (and others within the Centre), go to https://mathquest.edu.au/ and follow the links. What you will deliver:   Advance MathQuEST’s research programme on quantum algorithms for combinatorial optimisation. Design, analyse and implement novel quantum algorithms, producing high-quality publications in leading optimisation and quantum journals. Contribute to grant proposals, research reports and other scholarly outputs, supporting external and internal funding opportunities. Co-supervise and mentor postgraduate and junior research staff, fostering a collaborative, inclusive research environment. Engage with seminars, conferences, outreach and industry partners to disseminate findings and build strategic collaborations.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a completed or near completed PhD in mathematical optimisation, quantum optimisation, or a closely related discipline. Bring demonstrated experience developing, analysing and implementing quantum algorithms for discrete optimisation problems. Maintain a strong, emerging publication record and clear ambition to build an academic research career. Demonstrate excellent written and verbal communication skills, and confidence presenting research to varied audiences. Collaborate effectively within multi-disciplinary teams, engaging positively with colleagues from diverse backgrounds and institutions. Manage research projects with limited supervision, meeting deadlines and balancing competing priorities with strong organisation. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – The School of Mathematics and Statistics A globally recognised mathematical sciences community within the Faculty of Science, based at the University of Melbourne. The School hosts ARC Centres of Excellence, major training centres, and international collaborations across optimisation and quantum science. MathQuEST’s interdisciplinary network, supported by the IBM Quantum Hub, creates a dynamic environment for impactful quantum-era research careers. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Lindon Roberts, details in position description. For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 8 December, 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-020003 MathQuEST Postdoctoral Researcher on Quantum Optimisation_PD.pdf JR-020003 MathQuEST Postdoctoral Researcher on Quantum Optimisation_PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Post-doctoral-Researcher_JR-019490": {
      "title": "Post-doctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-09\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time (0.8 FTE); Fixed-term (3 years) Faculty: Faculty of Education School: School of Learning Sciences Salary: Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. (pro rata for part-time), plus 17% super Sector-shaping ARC Linkage project / longitudinal big data on student wellbeing / strong cross-institutional partnerships and mentoring Apply your quantitative expertise to drive evidence-based improvements in student wellbeing, engagement and learning across schooling. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead key research activities in a landmark ARC Linkage project on student wellbeing and engagement. You will collaborate with leading scholars, policy partners and schools, creating high-impact publications and actionable insights for practice. What you will deliver:   Plan and deliver longitudinal research activities, including data management, analysis, stakeholder engagement and coordinated project outputs. Apply advanced quantitative, longitudinal and person centred methods to large education datasets on wellbeing, engagement and learning. Lead and contribute to high quality publications, conference presentations, reports and funding proposals aligned with project goals. Coordinate research assistants and graduate students, providing guidance, feedback and effective workflows across project tasks. Represent the research program with partners and schools, strengthening networks, dissemination and impact across the education sector. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in psychology, social psychology, education, mental health or related social science, awarded within five years. Demonstrate a strong track record of peer-reviewed publications and scholarly outputs relative to career stage. Bring advanced quantitative skills, including longitudinal analysis and experience researching young people using large-scale datasets. Manage complex research projects effectively, showing excellent organisation, planning, time management and deadline-driven delivery. Collaborate productively within interdisciplinary teams and partnerships, including policy agencies, schools and research institutes. Bring theoretical knowledge of social determinants of health, social identity processes or youth mental health to understanding attitudes, behaviour and wellbeing. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – School of Learning Sciences   The School of Learning Sciences within the Faculty of Education is a dynamic centre for transformative education research, with particular strengths in student wellbeing, engagement and learning. The School leads major sector wide collaborations with the Victorian Department of Education, MCRI and CSIRO, harnessing longitudinal big data to inform practice and policy. Colleagues are highly collaborative, impact-focused and committed to equity, resilience and innovation, offering a rich intellectual environment and strong support for developing world-class, policy-relevant research. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Kate Reynolds, details in position description. For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 8 December, 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019490 Post-doctoral Researcher_PD.pdf JR-019490 Post-doctoral Researcher_PD.pdf"
    }
